3	### **1. A container is just a process running on your host OS, but:**\n\n- Isolated using Linux kernel features\n- Given:\n      - Its own filesystem view\n      - Its own process tree\n      - Its own network namespace\n\n**2. It still:**\n      - Uses the host kernel\n      - Is scheduled like any other process\n\n**3. ΓÇ£A container is a lightweight VMΓÇ¥ .** \n      - No. That belief causes 90% of Docker confusion.\n\n**4. How isolation works (important terms)**\n      - Namespaces\n\n            - PID namespace ΓåÆ its own process list\n            - Network namespace ΓåÆ its own IP\n            - Mount namespace ΓåÆ its own filesystem\n\n- cgroups\n\n            - Limit CPU\n            - Limit memory\n\n- chroot-like filesystem\n\n**5. Why this matters**\n\n      - Containers start in milliseconds (no OS boot)\n      - Containers can die if their main process dies\n      - Containers are not ΓÇ£machinesΓÇ¥ ΓÇö they are process wrappers	2026-02-19 07:42:26.923+00	1	4	What is docker?
