5	Each Dockerfile instruction creates a layer:\n  \n    /.dockerfile\n\n    FROM python:3.12-slim\n    COPY . /app\n    RUN pip install -r requirements.txt\n\nEach line:\n\n- Creates a filesystem snapshot\n- Cached independently\n\n### Docker build cache rules\n\n- If a layerΓÇÖs input doesnΓÇÖt change, Docker reuses it\n\n- If it changes ΓåÆ all following layers are rebuilt\n\nCommon mistake:\n\n    /.dockerfile\n    COPY . .\n    RUN pip install -r requirements.txt\n\n**Why this is bad:**\n\nAny code change invalidates dependency cache\n\nCorrect thinking:\n\n    COPY requirements.txt .\n    RUN pip install -r requirements.txt\n    COPY . .\n\n**Why this matters in real life ?**\n\n- CI pipelines become 10├ù faster\n- Faster iteration\n- Lower cloud costs	2026-02-24 09:44:54.153+00	2	4	How Image are Built
6	### 1. What Is a Docker Layer ΓÇö Really?\n\nEvery instruction in a Dockerfile creates a filesystem snapshot layer.\n\nExample:\n\n    FROM python:3.12-slim\n    WORKDIR /app\n    COPY requirements.txt .\n    RUN pip install -r requirements.txt\n    COPY . .\n\nEach of these creates a new read-only layer.\n\nThink of it like this:\n\n- Layer 1 ΓåÆ Base OS (python image)\n- Layer 2 ΓåÆ /app directory created\n- Layer 3 ΓåÆ requirements.txt copied\n- Layer 4 ΓåÆ dependencies installed\n- Layer 5 ΓåÆ source code copied\n\nThese layers stack on top of each other using a union filesystem.\n\n\n### 2. How Docker Caching Actually Works\n\nWhen you run:\n\n    docker build .\n\nDocker checks each instruction in order.\n\n**For every instruction:**\n\n- Has this exact instruction been run before?\n- Did its input files change?\n- If not ΓåÆ reuse cached layer.\n- If yes ΓåÆ rebuild this layer AND everything after it.\n\n**ΓÜá∩╕Å Important rule:**\n\n- Once one layer changes, all subsequent layers are rebuilt.\n\nThis is where people accidentally destroy performance.\n\n### 3. Why Layer Order Matters (CRITICAL)\n\nLetΓÇÖs compare two Dockerfiles.\n\nΓ¥î Bad Version\n\n    FROM python:3.12-slim\n    WORKDIR /app\n    COPY . .\n    RUN pip install -r requirements.txt\n\n**What happens?**\n\n- Any time you change ANY file\n- The COPY . . layer changes\n- That invalidates the cache\n- pip install runs again\n- Reinstalls ALL dependencies\n\nEven if you only changed one line of Python code.\n\nThis makes builds painfully slow.\n\nΓ£à Good Version\n\n    FROM python:3.12-slim\n    WORKDIR /app\n\n    COPY requirements.txt .\n    RUN pip install -r requirements.txt\n\n    COPY . .\n\nNow:\n\n- If you change app code ΓåÆ only last layer rebuilds\n- Dependencies remain cached\n- Build is fast\n\nThis is the most important Docker optimization rule.\n\n### 4∩╕ÅΓâú What Actually Invalidates Cache?\n\nDocker calculates a checksum for:\n\n- The instruction itself\n- The files involved in that instruction\n\nFor example:\n\n    COPY requirements.txt .\n\nIf:\n\n- requirements.txt changes ΓåÆ layer invalidated\n- Even whitespace change ΓåÆ invalidated\n\nBut:\n\n- If you change views.py ΓåÆ this layer stays cached.\n\n### 5∩╕ÅΓâú Hidden Cache Killers\n\nThese silently destroy build speed:\n\n**1∩╕ÅΓâú Copying Everything Too Early**\n\n    COPY . .\n\nThis includes:\n\n    .git\n    .env\n    node_modules\n    temp files\n\nSolution:\n\nUse .dockerignore.\n\n**2∩╕ÅΓâú Running apt-get update Wrong**\n\n    RUN apt-get update\n    RUN apt-get install -y gcc\n\nIf update layer cached ΓåÆ install may break later.\n\nCorrect:\n\n    RUN apt-get update && apt-get install -y gcc\n\nOne layer. Atomic.\n\n**3∩╕ÅΓâú Changing Order of Instructions**\n\nEven reordering lines breaks cache.\n\nDocker matches instruction hash exactly.\n\n### 6∩╕ÅΓâú How Layers Save Disk Space\n\nLayers are reusable across images.\n\nExample:\n\nTwo projects using:\n\n- FROM python:3.12-slim\n\nThey share the same base layer.\n\nDocker doesnΓÇÖt duplicate it.\n\nThis makes:\n\n- Image pulls faster\n\n- CI faster\n\nLocal dev lighter\n\n### 7∩╕ÅΓâú Why CI Becomes Slow Without Cache Strategy\n\nIn CI environments:\n\n- Every build often starts from scratch\n- Cache may not persist\n\nSolutions:\n\n- Remote layer caching\n- BuildKit\n- Pre-built base images\n\nIf you donΓÇÖt manage this:\n\n- Your pipeline goes from 1 minute ΓåÆ 12 minutes.\n\n### 8∩╕ÅΓâú Multi-Stage Builds & Cache\n\nExample:\n\n    FROM python:3.12 AS builder\n    RUN pip install -r requirements.txt\n\n    FROM python:3.12-slim\n    COPY --from=builder /usr/local/lib/python3.12/site-packages /site-packages\n\nEach stage:\n\n- Has its own layer cache\n- Can reuse previous builds\n\nThis reduces:\n\n- Final image size\n- Rebuild time\n\n### 9∩╕ÅΓâú Build Context ΓÇö The Silent Performance Killer\n\nWhen you run:\n\n    docker build .\n\nDocker sends the entire directory to the Docker daemon.\n\nIf your project has:\n\n    node_modules (300MB)\n    .git\n    logs\n    media uploads\n\nThey get sent unless ignored.\n\nThis slows build before it even starts.\n\nSolution:\n\n    .dockerignore\n\nExample:\n\n    .git\n    .env\n    node_modules\n    __pycache__\n    *.log\n    media/\n\nThis alone can reduce build time massively.	2026-03-04 04:33:09.648+00	1	4	Docker Layer and Caching
7	### 1∩╕ÅΓâú The High-Level Flow (Commit ΓåÆ Production)\n\nA typical deployment pipeline looks like this:\n\n    git push\n        Γåô\n    tests run\n        Γåô\n    build artifact\n        Γåô\n    store artifact\n        Γåô\n    deploy artifact\n        Γåô\n    run new version\n\nImportant idea:\n\n**Code is not deployed directly.\nArtifacts are deployed.**\n\nAn artifact is something built from the code (for example a Docker image).\n\n### 2∩╕ÅΓâú Why This Pipeline Exists\n\nWithout a pipeline, developers often deploy like this:\n\n    ssh server\n    git pull\n    pip install\n    restart service\n\nProblems:\n\n- different servers run different code\n- dependencies differ\n- debugging becomes impossible\n- rollback is hard\n\nA proper pipeline ensures:\n\n- reproducibility\n- consistency\n- traceability\n\n### 3∩╕ÅΓâú Step-by-Step Pipeline Flow\n\n**1. Step 1 ΓÇö git push**\n\nDeveloper commits code and pushes to repository.\n\nExample:\n\n    git add .\n    git commit -m "fix payment bug"\n    git push origin main\n\nThis triggers the pipeline.\n\nThink of it as:\n\n    code change\n        Γåô\n    pipeline triggered\n\n**2. Step 2 ΓÇö Automated Tests Run**\n\nBefore deploying anything, the pipeline verifies the code.\n\nTypical checks:\n\n- unit tests\n- integration tests\n- linting\n- formatting checks\n\nExample pipeline stage:\n\n    pytest\n\nWhy this matters:\n\nIf tests fail:\n\n    pipeline stops\n    deployment cancelled\n\nBroken code never reaches production.\n\n**3. Step 3 ΓÇö Build Artifact**\n\nNow the system builds the artifact.\n\nIn modern backend systems, the artifact is often a Docker image.\n\nExample:\n\n    docker build -t myapp:1.0 .\n\nThis image contains:\n\n- application code\n- runtime environment\n- dependencies\n\nImportant concept:\n\n- The artifact must be deterministic.\n\nMeaning:\n\n    same code\n      Γåô\n    same build process\n      Γåô\n    same artifact\n\n**4. Step 4 ΓÇö Store Artifact**\n\nThe built artifact must be stored somewhere accessible to deployment systems.\n\nFor Docker images, this is a container registry.\n\nExamples:\n\n- Docker Hub\n- AWS ECR\n- GitHub Container Registry\n- Google Artifact Registry\n\nPipeline step:\n\n    docker push myapp:1.0\n\nNow the artifact is stored centrally.\n\nServers can pull it when needed.\n\n**5. Step 5 ΓÇö Deploy Artifact**\n\nDeployment means running the artifact in production infrastructure.\n\nExample:\n\n    docker pull myapp:1.0\n    docker run myapp:1.0\n\nOr with container orchestration tools.\n\nKey principle:\n\n    servers never build artifacts\n    servers only run artifacts\n\nThis prevents environment differences.\n\n**6. Step 6 ΓÇö Restart Containers / Services**\n\nOnce the new artifact is available, the running service must switch to it.\n\nExample process:\n\n    old container running\n            Γåô\n    new container created\n            Γåô\n    traffic switched\n            Γåô\n    old container removed\n\nThis completes the deployment.\n\n### 4∩╕ÅΓâú Build vs Release vs Deploy\n\nThese terms are often confused.\n\nUnderstanding them is critical.\n\n**Build**\n\nBuild means creating the artifact.\n\nExample:\n\n    docker build myapp:1.0\n\nOutput:\n\n    Docker image\n\nNo servers involved yet.\n\n**Release**\n\nRelease means making the artifact available.\n\nExample:\n\n    docker push myapp:1.0\n\nNow the artifact is stored in a registry.\n\n**Deploy**\n\nDeploy means running the artifact in an environment.\n\nExample:\n\n    docker run myapp:1.0\n\nNow users interact with it.\n\n**Visual Flow**\n\n    Code\n     Γåô\n    Build\n     Γåô\n    Artifact\n     Γåô\n    Release\n     Γåô\n    Registry\n     Γåô\n    Deploy\n     Γåô\n    Running service\n\n### 5∩╕ÅΓâú Immutable Artifacts\n\nA critical DevOps concept.\n\nImmutable means:\n\n    artifact never changes after creation\n\nExample:\n\n    myapp:1.0\n\nThis image must never be modified.\n\nIf you need changes:\n\n    build new image\n    myapp:1.1\n\nWhy this matters:\n\n- reproducibility\n- debugging easier\n- rollback possible\n\n### 6∩╕ÅΓâú Rollback Strategy\n\nSometimes deployments break production.\n\nExample scenario:\n\n    deploy v2\n      Γåô\n    users report errors\n\nWith immutable artifacts, rollback is simple.\n\nJust run the previous version:\n\n    docker run myapp:1.0\n\nThis instantly restores the working system.\n\nWithout immutable artifacts, rollback becomes messy.\n\n### 7∩╕ÅΓâú Zero-Downtime Deployment (Concept)\n\nGoal:\n\n    deploy new version\n    without interrupting users\n\nNaive deployment:\n\n    stop old server\n    start new server\n\nUsers experience downtime.\n\nBetter approach:\n\n    start new version\n      Γåô\n    wait until healthy\n      Γåô\n    switch traffic\n      Γåô\n    stop old version\n\nThis ensures continuous service.\n\nCommon techniques:\n\n- blue-green deployment\n- rolling deployment\n- canary deployment\n\nYou don't need to implement these yet, just understand the concept.\n\n### 8∩╕ÅΓâú Why This Pipeline Reduces Fear\n\nMany developers fear deployment because they imagine something like:\n\n    push code\n    hope production survives\n\nBut a proper pipeline is deterministic:\n\n    commit\n      Γåô\n    tests\n      Γåô\n    build artifact\n      Γåô\n    store artifact\n      Γåô\n    deploy artifact\n\nEach step is controlled and repeatable.\n\n### 9∩╕ÅΓâú Manual Pipeline Practice (Important Exercise)\n\nYou should simulate the pipeline manually once.\n\n**1. Step 1 ΓÇö Build image**\n\n    docker build -t myapp:v1 .\n\n2**. Step 2 ΓÇö Tag image**\n\n    docker tag myapp:v1 myapp:latest\n\n*Tagging allows version control.*\n\n**3. Step 3 ΓÇö Run container**\n\n    docker run -d -p 8000:8000 myapp:v1\n\n*Your application is now running.*\n\n**4. Step 4 ΓÇö Build new version**\n\n*Modify code and build again:*\n\n    docker build -t myapp:v2 .\n\n**5. Step 5 ΓÇö Deploy new version**\n\n*Run the new container.*\n\n    docker run -d -p 8001:8000 myapp:v2\n\n*Verify it works.*\n\n**6. Step 6 ΓÇö Stop old container**\n\n    docker stop old_container\n\n*Now only the new version remains.*\n\n*You just performed a manual deployment pipeline.*\n\n### ≡ƒöƒ Mental Model of Modern Deployment\n\nModern backend deployment always follows this pattern:\n\n    Developer writes code\n        Γåô\n    Pipeline builds artifact\n        Γåô\n    Artifact stored in registry\n        Γåô\n    Infrastructure runs artifact\n\nInfrastructure never builds code.\n\nIt only runs already-built artifacts.\n\n### ≡ƒºá Final Insight\n\nDevOps tools like:\n\n- GitHub Actions\n- GitLab CI\n- Jenkins\n- Kubernetes\n\nare just automation layers over this exact flow.\n\nOnce you understand the flow:\n\n    commit ΓåÆ build ΓåÆ store ΓåÆ deploy\n\nDevOps tools become much less mysterious.	2026-03-05 11:18:25.681+00	2	4	Deployment Pipeline (From Commit ΓåÆ Production)
8	### 1∩╕ÅΓâú What CI/CD Actually Means\n\nCI/CD stands for two related but different ideas.\n\n**Continuous Integration (CI)**\n\nCI ensures that new code integrates safely with the existing codebase.\n\nGoal:\n\n    Every commit must be safe to merge.\n\nCI runs automatically when developers push code.\n\nTypical flow:\n\n    git push\n      Γåô\n    CI pipeline starts\n      Γåô\n    quality checks run\n      Γåô\n    pass ΓåÆ code accepted\n    fail ΓåÆ code rejected\n\nCI protects the codebase quality.\n\n**Continuous Delivery / Deployment (CD)**\n\nCD takes validated code and releases it to environments.\n\nGoal:\n\n    Safe and repeatable deployments.\n\nFlow:\n\n    code validated by CI\n      Γåô\n    artifact built\n      Γåô\n    artifact deployed\n      Γåô\n    application updated\n\nCD protects production stability.\n\n### 2∩╕ÅΓâú CI vs CD Responsibilities\n\nThink of them as two stages of the same pipeline.\n\n    CI ΓåÆ validates code\n    CD ΓåÆ releases code\n\n\n### 3∩╕ÅΓâú What CI Actually Checks\n\nCI is about verification and quality control.\n\nTypical CI responsibilities:\n\n**1∩╕ÅΓâú Linting**\n\nLinting ensures code style and static correctness.\n\nExample checks:\n\n- unused imports\n- syntax issues\n- formatting rules\n- code style violations\n\nExample Python tools:\n\n- flake8\n- ruff\n- black (formatting)\n\nExample pipeline step:\n\n    run linter\n    fail if style errors exist\n\nWhy this matters:\n\n- keeps codebase consistent\n- prevents trivial bugs\n\n**2∩╕ÅΓâú Tests**\n\nTests verify functional correctness.\n\nCommon test types:\n________________________________________________\n|  **Test Type**\t         |    **Purpose**                                       |\n|_______________________________________________ |\n|    Unit tests\t             |   verify small pieces of logic                  |\n|    Integration tests\t |  verify components working together    |\n|    API tests\t             | verify endpoints                                    |\n|_________________|______________________________|\n\nExample pipeline step:\n\n    pytest\n\nIf tests fail:\n\n    pipeline stops\n\nBroken code never moves forward.\n\n**3∩╕ÅΓâú Build**\n\nCI also verifies that the code can be built successfully.\n\nFor containerized applications this means:\n\n    docker build\n\nExample flow:\n\n    build docker image\n            Γåô\n    ensure dependencies install\n             Γåô\n    ensure application starts\n\nThis prevents deployment of *unbuildable applications.*\n\n**4∩╕ÅΓâú Security Scans (Concept)**\n\nModern pipelines include automated security checks.\n\nExamples:\n\n- dependency vulnerability scanning\n- container image scanning\n- secret detection\n\n\nCommon checks:\n\n    Is any dependency vulnerable?\n    Did someone commit a secret?\n    Is container image secure?\n\nThese reduce risk before deployment.\n\n### 4∩╕ÅΓâú When Pipelines Should Fail\n\nOne of the most important CI principles:\n\n- Fail early, fail automatically.\n\nPipeline must stop when:\n\n**Linter errors exist**\n\n    style violation\n          Γåô\n    pipeline fails\n\n**Tests fail**\n\n    feature breaks existing behavior\n          Γåô\n    tests fail\n          Γåô\n    pipeline stops\n\n**Build fails**\n\n    dependency broken\n          Γåô\n    image fails to build\n          Γåô\n    pipeline stops\n\n\n**Security vulnerability detected**\n\nExample:\n\n    dependency has critical vulnerability\n          Γåô\n    pipeline stops\n\nIf CI doesn't stop failures:\n\n    broken code reaches production\n\n\n### 5∩╕ÅΓâú CD Responsibilities\n\nOnce CI passes, the pipeline moves to deployment responsibilities.\n\nCD manages releasing the artifact safely.\n\nTypical CD tasks:\n\n**1∩╕ÅΓâú Deploy Application**\n\nCD pulls the artifact from the registry and runs it.\n\nExample conceptually:\n\n    pull docker image\n          Γåô\n    start new container\n\nThis updates the running application.\n\n**2∩╕ÅΓâú Run Database Migrations**\n\nBackend deployments often require database schema changes.\n\nExample migration:\n\n    add column to table\n    create new table\n    modify constraints\n\nTypical deployment order:\n\n    deploy code\n          Γåô\n    run migrations\n\nImportant note:\n\nBad migrations can break production.\n\nGood pipelines handle migrations carefully.\n\n**3∩╕ÅΓâú Restart Services**\n\nNew code must replace running services.\n\nTypical flow:\n\n    start new version\n          Γåô\n    verify health\n          Γåô\n    switch traffic\n          Γåô\n    stop old version\n\nThis ensures the application runs the new code.\n\n**4∩╕ÅΓâú Rollback on Failure**\n\nProduction systems must recover automatically from failed deployments.\n\nExample scenario:\n\n    deploy version v2\n            Γåô\n    health checks fail\n            Γåô\n    pipeline triggers rollback\n            Γåô\n    restore v1\n\nRollback strategies are critical for reliability.\n\n\n### **6∩╕ÅΓâú Pipeline Failure Points**\n\nYour pipeline should have clear stopping points.\n\nExample pipeline:\n\n    Commit\n      Γåô\n    Lint\n      Γåô\n    Test\n      Γåô\n    Build\n      Γåô\n    Security Scan\n      Γåô\n    Deploy\n\nFailures can occur at:\n\n    Lint failure ΓåÆ stop\n    Test failure ΓåÆ stop\n    Build failure ΓåÆ stop\n    Security failure ΓåÆ stop\n\nDeployment should never happen if CI fails.\n\n### 7∩╕ÅΓâú Human Approval Gates\n\nNot all deployments should be automatic.\n\nSome pipelines include manual approval steps.\n\nExample flow:\n\n    CI passes\n      Γåô\n    deploy to staging\n      Γåô\n    human approval\n      Γåô\n    deploy to production\n\nReasons:\n\n- verify staging environment\n- confirm release readiness\n- coordinate with teams\n\nLarge companies often require approvals before production deploys.\n\n### 8∩╕ÅΓâú Example Pipeline Design\n\nA realistic pipeline might look like:\n\n    Developer pushes code\n        Γåô\n    CI Stage\n          Γåô\n          Lint\n            Γåô\n          Tests\n            Γåô\n          Build Docker Image\n            Γåô\n          Security Scan\n        Γåô\n    Artifact stored in registry\n        Γåô\n    Deploy to staging\n        Γåô\n    Run migrations\n        Γåô\n    Integration tests\n        Γåô\n    Manual approval\n        Γåô\n    Deploy to production\n        Γåô\n    Health check\n        Γåô\n    Deployment complete\n\n### 9∩╕ÅΓâú Where Failures Stop the Flow\n\nLet's mark failure points:\n\n    push code\n    Γåô\n    lint  Γ¥î fail ΓåÆ stop\n    Γåô\n    tests Γ¥î fail ΓåÆ stop\n    Γåô\n    build Γ¥î fail ΓåÆ stop\n    Γåô\n    security scan Γ¥î fail ΓåÆ stop\n    Γåô\n    deploy staging Γ¥î fail ΓåÆ stop\n    Γåô\n    approval gate\n    Γåô\n    deploy production\n\nThis ensures unsafe changes never reach users.\n\n### ≡ƒöƒ Why Tool-Agnostic Thinking Matters\n\nDevelopers often think:\n\n    DevOps = learning CI/CD tools\n\nBut the truth is:\n\n    DevOps = designing reliable systems\n\nTools like:\n\n- GitHub Actions\n- GitLab CI\n- Jenkins\n\nonly implement the pipeline you design.\n\nIf you understand the flow:\n\n    validate ΓåÆ build ΓåÆ release ΓåÆ deploy\n\nSwitching tools becomes trivial.\n\n### ≡ƒºá Final Mental Model\n\nThink of CI/CD as a factory assembly line.\n\n    Code enters pipeline\n    Γåô\n    Quality checks\n    Γåô\n    Artifact built\n    Γåô\n    Artifact verified\n    Γåô\n    Artifact deployed\n    Γåô\n    Application updated\n\nIf any step detects a problem:\n\n    production is protected\n\nΓ£à If you truly understand CI/CD, you should be able to answer:\n\n- Why CI must run before merging code\n- Why builds must produce immutable artifacts\n- Why deployment should not happen if tests fail\n- Why staging exists before production	2026-03-08 07:16:38.015+00	2	4	What is CI/CD ?
9	### **1∩╕ÅΓâú The Critical Mindset Shift**\n\nIn development:\n\n    print("User created")\n\nYou look at your terminal and understand what happened.\n\nIn production:\n\n- thousands of requests\n- multiple servers\n- background workers\n- distributed services\n\nYou cannot SSH into servers every time something fails.\n\nInstead:\n\n    application\n    Γåô\n    generates logs\n    Γåô\n    logs stored centrally\n    Γåô\n    developers analyze them\n\nLogs become your debugger.\n\n### **2∩╕ÅΓâú What Logging Actually Is**\n\nLogging means recording structured information about application behavior.\n\nExample log:\n\n    {\n      "timestamp": "2026-03-08T10:20:15",\n      "level": "INFO",\n      "service": "api",\n      "message": "User login successful",\n      "user_id": 4521\n    }\n\nLogs help answer questions like:\n\n- What happened?\n- When did it happen?\n- Which request caused it?\n- Which service failed?\n\n### **3∩╕ÅΓâú Structured Logs**\n\nA common mistake is writing unstructured logs.\n\nBad log:\n\n    User login worked\n\nThis is difficult to search.\n\nStructured logs include fields.\n\nBetter log:\n\n    {\n      "event": "user_login",\n      "user_id": 4521,\n      "status": "success"\n    }\n\nAdvantages:\n\n- searchable\n- machine readable\n- easier analysis\n\nIn production systems, logs are usually stored in tools that allow searching and filtering.\n\n### **4∩╕ÅΓâú Log Levels**\n\nLog levels categorize how important a message is.\n\nTypical levels:\n\n1. DEBUG       ->     * detailed internal information*\n2. INFO\t      ->      *normal system behavior*\n3. WARNING ->     *something unusual*\n4. ERROR\t       ->    *failure occurred*\n5. CRITICAL\t->      *system failure*\n\n**DEBUG**\n\nUsed for deep debugging.\n\nExample:\n\n    logger.debug("Query returned %d rows", count)\n\nUsually disabled in production because it produces huge volumes of logs.\n\n**INFO**\n\nRecords normal application events.\n\nExample:\n\n    logger.info("User registered", extra={"user_id": user.id})\n\nShows high-level system activity.\n\n**WARNING**\n\nIndicates unexpected situations.\n\nExample:\n\n    logger.warning("Payment service timeout")\n\nSystem still works but something is wrong.\n\n**ERROR**\n\nIndicates failures.\n\nExample:\n\n    logger.error("Database connection failed")\n\nRequires investigation.\n\n**CRITICAL**\n\nUsed when the system is about to crash.\n\nExample:\n\n    logger.critical("Service cannot start")\n\nTriggers alerts immediately.\n\n### **5∩╕ÅΓâú Correlation IDs (Request IDs)**\n\nOne of the most powerful logging concepts.\n\nImagine a production system with thousands of requests per second.\n\nLogs look like this:\n\n1. request started\n2. db query\n3. cache miss\n4. payment API call\n5. request completed\n\nBut these logs belong to different users.\n\nWithout correlation IDs you cannot connect them.\n\n**Correlation ID Concept**\n\nEvery request gets a unique identifier.\n\nExample:\n\n    request_id=ab12cd\n\nAll logs for that request include the same ID.\n\nExample logs:\n\n    [ab12cd] request started\n    [ab12cd] fetching user\n    [ab12cd] calling payment service\n    [ab12cd] request completed\n\nNow you can reconstruct the entire request flow.\n\nThis becomes essential in microservices architectures.\n\n### **6∩╕ÅΓâú Logging in Django**\n\nA Django application usually produces several types of logs.\n\n1. Request Logs\n2. Record incoming requests.\n\nExample:\n\n    GET /api/orders 200 32ms\n\nUseful for:\n\n- traffic analysis\n- performance tracking\n- debugging API usage\n\n**Error Logs**\n\nRecord exceptions.\n\nExample:\n\n    ERROR Internal Server Error /api/orders\n    Traceback (most recent call last):\n    ...\n\nThese logs help diagnose application bugs.\n\n**Background Worker Logs (Celery)**\n\nMany backend systems use background workers.\n\nExample tasks:\n\n- sending emails\n- processing payments\n- generating reports\n\nCelery logs show task execution.\n\nExample:\n\n    Task send_email started\n    Task send_email completed\n\nIf a task fails:\n\n    Task payment_processing failed\n\nWithout worker logs, debugging async systems becomes impossible.\n\n### **7∩╕ÅΓâú Logs vs Metrics vs Traces**\n\nMonitoring systems collect three types of signals.\n\n1. **Logs**\n\nLogs record individual events.\n\nExample:\n\n    User login failed\n    Database connection error\n    Payment API timeout\n\nThey answer:\n\nWhat exactly happened?\n\n2. **Metrics**\n\nMetrics measure system performance numerically.\n\nExamples:\n\n- request count\n- error rate\n- CPU usage\n- memory usage\n- response time\n\nExample metric:\n\n    api_requests_total = 12000\n    error_rate = 0.2%\n\nMetrics answer:\n\nIs the system healthy?\n\n3. **Traces**\n\nTraces show how a request moves through multiple services.\n\nExample trace:\n\n    User request\n    Γåô\n    API service\n    Γåô\n    Auth service\n    Γåô\n    Payment service\n    Γåô\n    Database\n\nTracing helps find performance bottlenecks.\n\n### **8∩╕ÅΓâú Health Checks**\n\nHealth checks help systems determine if a service is functioning.\n\nExample endpoint:\n\n    /health\n\nResponse:\n\n    {\n      "status": "ok"\n    }\n\nHealth checks usually verify:\n\n- database connectivity\n- cache availability\n- external service status\n\nDeployment systems use health checks to determine if a container is ready.\n\n### **9∩╕ÅΓâú Alerts**\n\nMonitoring systems generate alerts when problems occur.\n\nExamples:\n\n**High error rate**\n\n    error_rate > 5%\n\n**High latency**\n\n    average_response_time > 2 seconds\n\n**Server overload**\n    \n    CPU usage > 90%\n\nAlerts notify engineers via:\n\n- email\n- messaging systems\n- incident tools\n\nWhen Should Alerts Trigger?\n\nAlerts should wake someone only for serious incidents.\n\nBad alert strategy:\n\n    alert for every warning\n\nEngineers get flooded and ignore alerts.\n\nGood strategy:\n\n    alert only for critical failures\n\nExamples:\n\n- API completely down\n- database unreachable\n- payment service failing\n\n### **≡ƒöƒ Real Production Debugging Scenario**\n\nExample issue:\n\nUsers report that checkout fails.\n\nMonitoring shows:\n\n    error_rate increased\n\nLogs show:\n\n    ERROR Payment API timeout\n\nYou discover:\n\n    external payment provider unavailable\n\nLogs + monitoring identified the problem quickly.\n\n### **1∩╕ÅΓâú1∩╕ÅΓâú Practice Task Explained**\n\nYou should add three logs to your system.\n\n**Request Start**\n\nLog when request begins.\n\nExample:\n\n    INFO request started\n    method=POST\n    path=/api/orders\n\n**Request End**\n\nLog when request completes.\n\nExample:\n\n    INFO request completed\n    status=200\n    duration=45ms\n\nThis helps detect slow endpoints.\n\n**Error Logs**\n\nCapture exceptions.\n\nExample:\n\n    ERROR database connection failed\n\n### **1∩╕ÅΓâú2∩╕ÅΓâú Simulated Failure Exercise**\n\nNow simulate a failure.\n\nExample:\n\n    kill database container\n\nThen call an API endpoint.\n\nLogs should show:\n\n    ERROR database connection refused\n\nIf logs clearly explain the issue you can debug production remotely.\n\n### **1∩╕ÅΓâú3∩╕ÅΓâú Senior Developer Skill**\n\nA senior backend developer should be able to:\n\n- identify production issues using logs\n- analyze metrics for performance problems\n- detect failures before users report them\n\nThe real skill is:\n\n- debugging without direct server access\n\nIf you can diagnose production problems only using logs and monitoring dashboards, you have reached a very important milestone in backend engineering.	2026-03-10 03:22:52.56+00	1	4	Logging and Monitoring
10	Scaling is where software design meets real-world traffic. Many systems work perfectly for 10 users but collapse with 10,000 users. Understanding scaling basics helps you design systems that grow safely instead of breaking suddenly. ≡ƒôê\n\n### **1∩╕ÅΓâú What Scaling Actually Means**\n\nScaling means handling more workload without system failure.\n\nWorkload may increase due to:\n\n- more users\n- more API requests\n- larger datasets\n- background jobs\n\nExample:\n\n    100 requests/sec ΓåÆ system OK\n    5000 requests/sec ΓåÆ system crashes\n\nScaling ensures the system can handle the larger load.\n\n### **2∩╕ÅΓâú Two Types of Scaling**\n\nThere are two fundamental ways to scale a system.\n\n1. **Vertical Scaling (Scale Up)**\n\nIncrease the power of a single machine.\n\nExample:\n\n    2 CPU ΓåÆ 8 CPU\n    8GB RAM ΓåÆ 64GB RAM\n\nDiagram:\n\n    Before\n    [ Server ]\n    \n    After\n    [ Bigger Server ]\n\nAdvantages:\n\nsi\n\n- mple\n- no architecture change\n\nProblems:\n\n- hardware limits\n- expensive\n- single point of failure\n\nEventually one machine cannot grow further.\n\n2. **Horizontal Scaling (Scale Out)**\n\nAdd more machines instead of making one machine bigger.\n\nExample:\n\n    1 server ΓåÆ 5 servers\n\nDiagram:\n\n        Load Balancer\n        /   |   \\\n     Server Server Server\n\nAdvantages:\n\n- almost unlimited scaling\n- redundancy\n- high availability\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n- requires distributed architecture\n- introduces new complexity\n\nModern web systems rely heavily on horizontal scaling.\n\n### **3∩╕ÅΓâú Load Balancing**\n\nWhen multiple servers exist, incoming traffic must be distributed.\n\nA load balancer sits in front of servers.\n\n    User Requests\n           Γåô\n       Load Balancer\n       /     |     \\\n    Server1 Server2 Server3\n\nResponsibilities:\n\n- distribute traffic evenly\n- detect failed servers\n- redirect traffic away from unhealthy instances\n\nCommon strategies:\n\n1. **Round Robin**\n\nRequests distributed sequentially:\n\n    Req1 ΓåÆ server1\n    Req2 ΓåÆ server2\n    Req3 ΓåÆ server3\n    Req4 ΓåÆ server1\n\n2. **Least Connections**\n\nSend request to server handling the fewest active requests.\n\n### **4∩╕ÅΓâú Stateless Services (Critical for Scaling)**\n\nStateless means:\n\n-> Each request contains all information needed to process it.\n\nThe server does not rely on local memory.\n\nExample stateless request:\n\n    GET /orders\n    Authorization: Bearer <token>\n\nThe server can handle the request independently.\n\n**Why Stateless Matters**\n\nIf servers store session data locally:\n\n- User logs in ΓåÆ session stored in server1 memory\n\nNext request might go to server2:\n\n- server2 cannot find session\n\nUser appears logged out.\n\nStateless design solves this.\n\nSession data should be stored in:\n\n- databases\n- distributed caches\n- JWT tokens\n\n### **5∩╕ÅΓâú Shared State Systems**\n\nIn scalable architectures, state is stored in shared infrastructure.\n\nTypical architecture:\n\n           Load Balancer\n             /      \\\n        App Server  App Server\n             \\      /\n             Database\n               |\n              Cache\n\nServers become interchangeable workers.\n\nThey can be created or destroyed without affecting data.\n\n### **6∩╕ÅΓâú Common Scaling Bottlenecks**\n\nSystems rarely fail randomly. They fail due to specific bottlenecks.\n\n**CPU Bottleneck**\n\nHeavy computation tasks overwhelm CPU.\n\nExample:\n\n- video processing\n- encryption\n- large data transformations\n\nSolution:\n\n- scale horizontally\n- move work to background jobs\n\n**Database Bottleneck**\n\nDatabases often become the first scaling limit.\n\nSymptoms:\n\n- slow queries\n- high connection counts\n- query locks\n\nSolutions:\n\n- query optimization\n- caching\n- read replicas\n\n**Network Bottleneck**\n\nToo much traffic between services.\n\nExample:\n\n- microservice calls microservice excessively\n\nSolution:\n\n- reduce unnecessary requests\n- batch operations\n- caching\n\n### **7∩╕ÅΓâú Caching (One of the Most Powerful Scaling Tools)**\n\nCaching stores frequently requested data in fast storage.\n\nExample:\n\n- User profile requested 1000 times\n\nInstead of hitting database every time:\n\n    request\n    Γåô\n    cache hit\n    Γåô\n    return result\n\nCommon caching systems include distributed caches like Redis.\n\nBenefits:\n\n- faster responses\n- lower database load\n- improved scalability\n\n### **8∩╕ÅΓâú Background Jobs**\n\nSome tasks should not block user requests.\n\nExamples:\n\n- sending emails\n- generating reports\n- image processing\n- payment verification\n\nInstead of handling inside request:\n\n    User request\n    Γåô\n    enqueue job\n    Γåô\n    worker processes later\n\nWorkers process tasks asynchronously.\n\nPython systems often use job queues like Celery.\n\nThis prevents API servers from being overloaded.\n\n### **9∩╕ÅΓâú Why Scaling Breaks Applications**\n\nMany systems fail under scale because they assume single server behavior.\n\nExamples:\n\n1. **Local file storage**\n\n    Server1 saves file\n    Server2 cannot access it\n\nSolution:\n\n- shared storage\n- object storage systems\n\n2. **In-memory cache**\n\n    Server1 cache contains value\n    Server2 cache does not\n\nSolution:\n\n- distributed cache like Redis.\n\n3. **Cron jobs**\n\nMultiple servers may execute the same scheduled task.\n\nExample:\n\n    5 servers run nightly cleanup\n    ΓåÆ task executed 5 times\n\nSolution:\n\n- distributed task scheduling\n- dedicated worker systems\n\n### **≡ƒöƒ Autoscaling**\n\nModern cloud systems can automatically adjust capacity.\n\nExample behavior:\n\n    traffic increases\n    Γåô\n    new servers start automatically\n\nWhen traffic drops:\n\n- servers shut down\n\nAutoscaling ensures resources match demand.\n\n### **1∩╕ÅΓâú1∩╕ÅΓâú Scaling Failure Scenario**\n\nExample real-world failure:\n\nTraffic spike during a product launch.\n\n10x normal traffic\n\nWhat happens:\n\n- API servers overwhelmed\n- database overloaded\n- timeouts increase\n- users receive errors\n\nProper scaling architecture prevents this.\n\n### **1∩╕ÅΓâú2∩╕ÅΓâú Practical Scaling Exercise**\n\nTo understand scaling behavior:\n\nRun two containers of your API.\n\nExample conceptually:\n\n    docker run api\n    docker run api\n\nAdd a load balancer.\n\nSend requests repeatedly.\n\nObserve:\n\n1. response times\n2. request distribution\n3. failure behavior\n\nThen simulate failures:\n\n*-> stop one server*\n\nVerify if the system continues working.\n\nThis demonstrates resilience through scaling.\n\n### ≡ƒºá Final Mental Model\n\nA scalable system typically looks like:\n\n              Load Balancer\n                /      \\\n        App Server    App Server\n             |           |\n             +----Cache--+\n                   |\n               Database\n                   |\n            Background Workers\n\nKey principles:\n\n1. stateless application servers\n2. centralized state storage\n3. asynchronous workloads\n4. horizontal scaling	2026-03-10 05:12:52.697+00	2	4	Scaling basics
11	### 1. Docker Image Layers ΓÇö How They Stack\n\nA Docker image is not one big file.\n\nInstead, it is made of layers stacked on top of each other.\n\nThink of it like this:\n\n    Application code\n    -----------------\n    Dependencies layer\n    -----------------\n    Python runtime\n    -----------------\n    Base OS (Ubuntu/Alpine)\n\nEach layer is read-only.\n\nExample image stack:\n\n    Layer 4 ΓåÆ your app\n    Layer 3 ΓåÆ pip installed packages\n    Layer 2 ΓåÆ python runtime\n    Layer 1 ΓåÆ base linux image\n\nEach instruction in a Dockerfile creates a new layer.\n\nExample Dockerfile:\n\n    FROM python:3.11\n    WORKDIR /app\n    COPY requirements.txt .\n    RUN pip install -r requirements.txt\n    COPY . .\n\nLayer breakdown:\n\n    Layer 1 ΓåÆ python:3.11\n    Layer 2 ΓåÆ WORKDIR /app\n    Layer 3 ΓåÆ COPY requirements.txt\n    Layer 4 ΓåÆ RUN pip install\n    Layer 5 ΓåÆ COPY source code\n\n----------------------------\n\n**Why Layers Exist**\n\nLayers provide 3 major advantages.\n\n**1. Reusability**\n\nIf two images share the same base layer:\n\n    python:3.11\n\nDocker downloads it only once.\n\n**2. Faster Builds**\n\nIf you change only your source code, Docker reuses earlier layers.\n\nExample:\n\n    pip install dependencies ΓåÆ cached\n    only source code layer rebuilt\n\nThis dramatically speeds up builds.\n\n**3. Efficient Storage**\n\nLayers are deduplicated.\n\nMany containers can share the same image layers.\n\n**Copy-on-Write (Important)**\n\nImages are read-only.\n\nWhen a container starts, Docker adds one writable layer on top.\n\n    Image layers (read-only)\n    ------------------------\n    Container layer (writeable)\n\nIf your app writes a file:\n\n    /tmp/log.txt\n\nIt goes into the container layer, not the image.\n\nWhen the container is deleted ΓåÆ that data disappears.\n\nThis is why volumes exist (you'll learn later).\n\n### 2. Image Registry\n\nImages are stored in registries.\n\nA registry is basically:\n\n- A server that stores docker images\n\nThe most common one is:\n\n**Docker Hub**\n\nWhen you run:\n\n    docker run nginx\n\nDocker automatically pulls:\n\n- nginx image from Docker Hub\n- Public vs Private Registries\n- Public registry\n\nAnyone can download images.\n\nExamples:\n\n- Docker Hub\n- GitHub Container Registry\n\nExample public image:\n\n- nginx\n- postgres\n- redis\n- python\n- node\n\n**Private registry**\n\nOnly your team/company can access it.\n\nUsed for:\n\n    proprietary applications\n    internal services\n    private images\n\nExamples:\n\n- Amazon Elastic Container Registry\n- Google Artifact Registry\n\n### 3. Pulling, Tagging, and Inspecting Images\n\n### Pulling Images\n\nDownload image manually:\n\n    docker pull nginx\n\nThis downloads:\n\n    nginx:latest\n\nDefault tag = *latest.*\n\n**Image Tagging**\n\nImages have this format:\n\n    repository:tag\n\nExample:\n\n    nginx:latest\n    postgres:15\n    python:3.11\n\nTag represents version.\n\nExample:\n\n    docker pull postgres:15\n\nThis downloads PostgreSQL version 15.\n\n**Tagging Your Own Images**\n\nYou can rename images.\n\nExample:\n\n    docker tag myapp:latest myrepo/myapp:v1\n\nNow the image name becomes:\n\n    myrepo/myapp:v1\n\nThis is used before pushing to registry.\n\n### Inspecting Images\n\nList images:\n\n    docker images\n\nExample output:\n\n    REPOSITORY   TAG       IMAGE ID\n    nginx        latest    a875871...\n    python       3.11      b573211...\n\nTo see deeper info:\n\n    docker inspect nginx\n\nYou can see:\n\n    image layers\n    environment variables\n    entrypoint\n    configuration\n\n### 4. Container Lifecycle\n\nContainers follow a lifecycle.\n\n    create ΓåÆ start ΓåÆ stop ΓåÆ remove\n\nLetΓÇÖs break it down.\n\n**Create Container**\n\nCreates container but does not start it.\n\n    docker create nginx\n\nContainer exists but isn't running.\n\n**Start Container**\n\n    docker start <container-id>\n\nNow the process begins running.\n\n**Stop Container**\n\n    docker stop <container-id>\n\nGracefully stops the process.\n\n**Remove Container**\n\n    docker rm <container-id>\n\nDeletes container completely.\n\n**Shortcut Command**\n\nMost people use:\n\n    docker run nginx\n\nThis does:\n\n    pull ΓåÆ create ΓåÆ start\n\nAll in one command.\n\n### 5. Interactive Mode (-it) vs Detached Mode (-d)\n\nContainers can run in two main modes.\n\n**Interactive Mode (-it)**\n\nExample:\n\n    docker run -it ubuntu bash\n\nFlags meaning:\n\n    -i ΓåÆ interactive input\n    -t ΓåÆ terminal\n\nThis gives you a shell inside container.\n\nExample result:\n\n    root@container:/#\n\nYou are now inside the container OS.\n\nYou can run:\n\n    ls\n    apt update\n    cat /etc/os-release\n\nExit:\n\n    exit\n\nContainer stops.\n\n**Detached Mode (-d)**\n\nRuns container in background.\n\nExample:\n\n    docker run -d nginx\n\nNow container runs silently.\n\nCheck running containers:\n\n    docker ps\n\nYou will see nginx running.\n\nWhen Each Mode Is Used\n\nInteractive:\n\n    debugging\n    testing\n    learning\n\nDetached:\n\n    servers\n    databases\n    web apps\n    production services\n\nExample backend services usually run detached.\n\n### 6. Port Mapping (-p) and Container Naming\n\nContainers have isolated networks.\n\nIf a service runs inside container:\n\n    nginx listening on port 80\n\nYour computer cannot access it directly.\n\n**Port Mapping**\n\nUse -p.\n\nExample:\n\n    docker run -p 8080:80 nginx\n\nMeaning:\n\n    host_port : container_port\n\nSo:\n\n    localhost:8080 ΓåÆ container:80\n\nNow you can open:\n\n    http://localhost:8080\n\nAnd see nginx page.\n\n**Naming Containers**\n\nWithout a name:\n\n    docker run nginx\n\nDocker assigns random names like:\n\n    hopeful_panda\n    nostalgic_einstein\n\nInstead use:\n\n    docker run --name my-nginx nginx\n\nNow container name is:\n\n    my-nginx\n\nYou can control it easily.\n\nExample:\n\n    docker stop my-nginx\n    docker rm my-nginx\n\n### Practical Exercise (Very Important)\n\nRun this sequence:\n\n    docker pull nginx\n\nThen:\n\n    docker run -d -p 8080:80 --name webserver nginx\n\nCheck running containers:\n\n    docker ps\n\nOpen browser:\n\n    http://localhost:8080\n\nStop container:\n\n    docker stop webserver\n\nRemove container:\n\n    docker rm webserver\n\n### After Day 2\n\nYou should now understand:\n\n    Dockerfile ΓåÆ builds image\n    Image ΓåÆ blueprint\n    Container ΓåÆ running instance\n    Registry ΓåÆ image storage\n    Layers ΓåÆ efficient image structure\n\nIf you want, I can also show you Day 3, which is where Docker becomes useful for backend developers:\n\n    Dockerfile creation\n    Building images\n    Running your own application\n    Docker build cache\n    Best Dockerfile practices\n\nThis is where you'll Dockerize a Django project.	2026-03-13 09:25:09.127+00	2	6	Day 1: Docker Basics
12	### 1. Dockerfile Syntax & Instruction Set\n\nA Dockerfile is simply a text file that contains instructions to build a Docker image.\n\nThink of it like:\n\n    Recipe ΓåÆ build image\n\nExample Dockerfile:\n\n    FROM python:3.11\n    WORKDIR /app\n    COPY requirements.txt .\n    RUN pip install -r requirements.txt\n    COPY . .\n    CMD ["python", "app.py"]\n\nEach instruction creates a new image layer.\n\nDocker executes the instructions from top to bottom.\n\nProcess:\n\n    Dockerfile\n       Γåô\n    docker build\n       Γåô\n    Image created\n       Γåô\n    Container runs from image\n\n### 2. Important Dockerfile Instructions\n\nThese are the core instructions every Dockerfile uses.\n\n--------\n\n**FROM**\n\nFROM defines the base image.\n\nEvery Docker image starts from another image.\n\nExample:\n\n    FROM python:3.11\n\nThis means:\n\n    Base OS + Python runtime already installed\n\nYou donΓÇÖt need to install Python manually.\n\nCommon base images:\n\n    python\n    node\n    nginx\n    ubuntu\n    alpine\n\nExample minimal base:\n\n    FROM alpine\n\n'alpine' is a very small Linux image.\n\n--------\n\n**RUN**\n\nRUN executes commands while building the image.\n\nExample:\n\n    RUN pip install django\n\nOr:\n\n    RUN apt-get update && apt-get install -y git\n\nImportant:\n\nRUN executes during build\n\nNot when container runs.\n\nExample:\n\n    RUN pip install -r requirements.txt\n\nThis installs dependencies into the image.\n\n--------\n\n**COPY**\n\nCOPY copies files from host machine ΓåÆ image filesystem.\n\nExample:\n\n    COPY . .\n\nMeaning:\n\nCurrent folder ΓåÆ container working directory\n\nExample:\n\n    COPY requirements.txt /app/\n\nThis copies only that file.\n\n--------\n\n**ADD**\n\nADD is similar to COPY, but with extra features.\n\nExample:\n\n    ADD archive.tar.gz /app\n\nDocker automatically extracts archives.\n\nIt can also download URLs.\n\nBut best practice:\n\nPrefer COPY unless you need ADD features\n\n--------\n\n**WORKDIR**\n\nWORKDIR sets the working directory inside container.\n\nExample:\n\n    WORKDIR /app\n\nAll following commands run in that folder.\n\nEquivalent to:\n\n    cd /app\n\nBut permanent.\n\nExample:\n\n    WORKDIR /app\n    COPY . .\n\nNow files go into /app.\n\n------\n\n**EXPOSE**\n\nEXPOSE documents which port the container uses.\n\nExample:\n\n    EXPOSE 8000\n\nThis tells Docker:\n\nApplication listens on port 8000\n\nImportant:\n\nEXPOSE does NOT publish the port\n\nActual port mapping happens with:\n\n    docker run -p\n\n--------\n\n**CMD**\n\nCMD defines the default command when container starts.\n\nExample:\n\n    CMD ["python", "app.py"]\n\nWhen container runs:\n\n    python app.py\n\nIf user provides another command, it overrides CMD.\n\nExample:\n\n    docker run myapp bash\n\nNow bash runs instead of python app.py.\n\n--------\n\n**ENTRYPOINT**\n\nENTRYPOINT defines the main executable for the container.\n\nExample:\n\n    ENTRYPOINT ["python"]\n\nNow the container always runs Python.\n\nAdditional commands become arguments.\n\nExample:\n\n    docker run myimage script.py\n\nActually runs:\n\n    python script.py\n\n### **3. CMD vs ENTRYPOINT** *(Key Difference)*\n\nThis is a very common interview question.\n\n**CMD**\n\nDefines default command.\n\nBut user can override it.\n\nExample Dockerfile:\n\n    CMD ["python", "app.py"]\n\nRun normally:\n\n    docker run myapp\n\nRuns:\n\n    python app.py\n\nOverride:\n\n    docker run myapp bash\n\nRuns:\n\n    bash\n\n--------\n\n**ENTRYPOINT**\n\nDefines fixed executable.\n\nExample:\n\n    ENTRYPOINT ["python"]\n    CMD ["app.py"]\n\nNow behavior:\n\n    docker run myapp\n\nRuns:\n\n    python app.py\n\nBut if user runs:\n\n    docker run myapp test.py\n\nActual command:\n\n    python test.py\n    Simple Mental Model\n    ENTRYPOINT ΓåÆ fixed program\n    CMD ΓåÆ default arguments\n\n### 4. Build Context and .dockerignore\n\nWhen you run:\n\n    docker build .\n\nDocker sends the entire folder to the Docker daemon.\n\nThis folder is called the build context.\n\nExample context:\n\n    project/\n     Γö£ app.py\n     Γö£ requirements.txt\n     Γö£ node_modules\n     Γö£ .git\n     Γö£ Dockerfile\n\nEverything gets sent.\n\nProblem:\n\nHuge folders slow down builds\n\nExample:\n\n    node_modules\n    .git\n    logs\n\nThese should not be included.\n\n    .dockerignore\n\nWorks exactly like .gitignore.\n\nExample:\n\n    node_modules\n    .git\n    *.log\n    __pycache__\n\nNow Docker ignores these files during build.\n\nBenefits:\n\n- faster builds\n- smaller images\n- less data transfer\n\n### 5. Building Your Image\n\nCommand:\n\n    docker build -t myapp .\n\nMeaning:\n\n    -t ΓåÆ tag image\n    myapp ΓåÆ image name\n    . ΓåÆ build context\n\nExample output:\n\n    Step 1/5 : FROM python:3.11\n    Step 2/5 : WORKDIR /app\n    Step 3/5 : COPY requirements.txt .\n    Step 4/5 : RUN pip install\n    Step 5/5 : COPY . .\n\nNow check images:\n\n    docker images\n\nYou should see:\n\n    myapp\n\nRun container:\n\n    docker run myapp\n\n### 6. Layer Caching (Why Order Matters)\n\nDocker caches layers to speed up builds.\n\nExample Dockerfile:\n\n    FROM python:3.11\n    COPY . .\n    RUN pip install -r requirements.txt\n\nProblem:\n\nIf any file changes, Docker must rebuild everything.\n\nEven dependencies.\n\nSlow build.\n\nOptimized Dockerfile\n\n    FROM python:3.11\n\n    WORKDIR /app\n    \n    COPY requirements.txt .\n    \n    RUN pip install -r requirements.txt\n    \n    COPY . .\n\nNow flow:\n\n- [ ] copy requirements\n- [ ] install dependencies\n- [ ] copy source code\n\nIf only source code changes:\n\ndependency layer cached\n\nBuild becomes much faster.\n\nBad vs Good Layer Order\n\nBad:\n\n    COPY . .\n    RUN pip install\n\nGood:\n\n    COPY requirements.txt .\n    RUN pip install\n    COPY . .\n\n**Example Complete Dockerfile (Backend API)**\n\nExample for a simple **Django API:\n\n    FROM python:3.11\n    \n    WORKDIR /app\n    \n    COPY requirements.txt .\n    \n    RUN pip install -r requirements.txt\n    \n    COPY . .\n    \n    EXPOSE 8000\n\n    CMD ["python", "manage.py", "runserver", "0.0.0.0:8000"]\n\nBuild:\n\n    docker build -t django-api .\n\nRun:\n\n    docker run -p 8000:8000 django-api\n\nOpen browser:\n\n    http://localhost:8000	2026-03-14 06:59:13.069+00	2	6	Day 2: DockerFile
13	### 1. Why Containers Are Ephemeral by Default\n\nContainers are designed to be temporary and disposable.\n\nWhen you run a container:\n\n    docker run nginx\n\nDocker creates a writable container layer on top of the image.\n\nArchitecture:\n\n    Image Layers (read-only)\n    -----------------------\n    Container Writable Layer\n\nAny file changes go into the container layer.\n\nExample:\n\n    /var/log/app.log\n    /tmp/data.txt\n\nBut if the container is removed:\n\n    docker rm container_id\n\nEverything inside the writable layer is deleted.\n\nThis is why containers are called ephemeral.\n\nMeaning:\n\n- Temporary\n- Disposable\n- Stateless\n- Example Problem\n\nSuppose you run a database container:\n\n    docker run postgres\n\nInsert some data:\n\n1. users table\n2. orders table\n\nNow remove container:\n\n    docker rm postgres_container\n\nResult:\n\n***ALL DATA GONE***\n\nThis is unacceptable for databases.\n\nSolution:\n\n**Volumes**\n\n### 2. Bind Mounts vs Named Volumes vs tmpfs\n\nDocker provides three storage mechanisms.\n\n1. Bind Mount\n2. Named Volume\n3. tmpfs\n\nEach has different use cases.\n\n**1. Bind Mounts**\n\nBind mounts map a host machine folder ΓåÆ container folder.\n\nExample:\n\n    docker run -v /host/data:/container/data nginx\n\nMeaning:\n\n    Host directory ΓåÆ /host/data\n    Container directory ΓåÆ /container/data\n\nAny file written inside container appears on host.\n\nExample:\n\nContainer writes:\n\n    /container/data/file.txt\n\nYou can see it on host:\n\n    /host/data/file.txt\n\nBind Mount Use Cases\n\nBind mounts are commonly used for:\n\n- development environments\n- live code editing\n- configuration files\n\nExample for backend dev:\n\n    docker run -v $(pwd):/app python\n\nYour source code is shared with container.\n\nProblems With Bind Mounts\n\n- They depend on host filesystem.\n\nIssues:\n\n- OS-specific paths\n- permission issues\n- not portable\n\nBecause of this, production setups often prefer named volumes.\n\n**2. Named Volumes**\n\nNamed volumes are managed entirely by Docker.\n\nExample:\n\n    docker volume create postgres_data\n\nDocker stores volume somewhere internally:\n\n    /var/lib/docker/volumes/\n\nBut you donΓÇÖt manage it directly.\n\nRun container:\n\n    docker run -v postgres_data:/var/lib/postgresql/data postgres\n\nMeaning:\n\n    Named volume ΓåÆ postgres_data\n    Mounted inside ΓåÆ database data directory\n\nNow if container stops or is deleted:\n\n    docker rm postgres_container\n\nThe volume still exists.\n\nData persists.\n\n***Why Named Volumes Are Preferred ?***\n\nAdvantages:\n\n- portable\n- managed by docker\n- safer for production\n- easier backups\n\n**3. tmpfs**\n\ntmpfs is in-memory storage.\n\nExample:\n\n    docker run --tmpfs /temp nginx\n\n*"" Data stored in RAM not on disk. ""*\n\nUse cases:\n\n- sensitive data\n- temporary cache\n- high-speed operations\n\nBut:\n\ndata disappears when container stops\n\nSummary:\n\nBind mount ΓåÆ host folder mapping\nNamed volume ΓåÆ docker-managed storage\ntmpfs ΓåÆ memory storage\n\n### 3. Creating and Managing Named Volumes\n\nCreate volume:\n\n    docker volume create myvolume\n\nCheck volumes:\n\n    docker volume ls\n\nExample output:\n\n    DRIVER    VOLUME NAME\n    local       myvolume\n    local       postgres_data\n\nInspect volume:\n\n    docker volume inspect myvolume\n\nOutput shows:\n\n    mountpoint\n    driver\n    metadata\n\nRemove volume:\n\n    docker volume rm myvolume\n\nImportant:\n\nYou cannot remove volume if itΓÇÖs in use by a container.\n\n### 4. Mount Host Directories into Containers\n\nBind mount example:\n\n    docker run -v /home/user/app:/app python\n\nThis means:\n\n    host folder ΓåÆ /home/user/app\n    container folder ΓåÆ /app\n\nNow container sees the same files.\n\nExample:\n\nHost file:\n\n    app.py\n\nInside container:\n\n    /app/app.py\n\nThis is extremely useful for development.\n\nExample with Django:\n\n    docker run -v $(pwd):/app -p 8000:8000 django-image\n\nYou edit code locally ΓåÆ container sees changes instantly.\n\n### 5. Volume Drivers\n\nDocker volumes use drivers.\n\nDefault driver:\n\n    local\n\nThis stores data on the host filesystem.\n\nBut Docker also supports external storage systems.\n\nExamples:\n\n- NFS\n- Amazon EFS\n- Ceph\n- Azure File Storage\n\nThese allow containers across multiple servers to share data.\n\nExample driver usage:\n\n    docker volume create \\\n      --driver local \\\n      myvolume\n\nIn large systems (like Kubernetes), advanced drivers enable:\n\n- distributed storage\n- cloud storage integration\n- high availability\n\nBut for most local development:\n\ndefault local driver is enough\n\n### 6. Backup and Restore Volume Data\n\nVolumes store critical data, especially databases.\n\nYou should know how to back them up.\n\nExample backup using a temporary container.\n\n**Backup:**\n\n    docker run --rm \\\n      -v postgres_data:/volume \\\n      -v $(pwd):/backup \\\n      ubuntu \\\n      tar czf /backup/backup.tar.gz /volume\n\nThis:\n\n- mounts volume\n- compresses data\n- saves backup to host\n\n**Restore Backup**\n\n    docker run --rm \\\n      -v postgres_data:/volume \\\n      -v $(pwd):/backup \\\n      ubuntu \\\n      tar xzf /backup/backup.tar.gz -C /\n\nNow volume data is restored.\n\nThis technique is commonly used in production.\n\n### Hands-On Project (Very Important)\n\nRun **PostgreSQL with persistent storage.\n\n**Step 1 ΓÇö Create Volume**\n\n  docker volume create pgdata\n\n**Step 2 ΓÇö Run PostgreSQL Container**\n\n    docker run -d \\\n      --name postgres-db \\\n      -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=secret \\\n      -v pgdata:/var/lib/postgresql/data \\\n      -p 5432:5432 \\\n      postgres\n\nExplanation:\n\n*-v pgdata:/var/lib/postgresql/data* -> Mounts volume to PostgreSQL data directory.\n\n**Step 3 ΓÇö Insert Some Data**\n\nConnect using:\n\n    docker exec -it postgres-db psql -U postgres\n\nCreate table:\n\n    CREATE TABLE test(id INT);\n    INSERT INTO test VALUES(1);\n\n**Step 4 ΓÇö Stop Container**\n\n    docker stop postgres-db\n\n**Step 5 ΓÇö Remove Container**\n\n    docker rm postgres-db\n\n**Step 6 ΓÇö Start Again**\n\n    docker run -d \\\n      --name postgres-db \\\n      -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=secret \\\n      -v pgdata:/var/lib/postgresql/data \\\n      -p 5432:5432 \\\n      postgres\n\nNow check table again.\n\nYour data still exists.\n\nBecause the volume persisted.\n\n**New Command Syntax: --mount**\n\nModern Docker prefers --mount.\n\nExample:\n\n    docker run \\\n    --mount source=pgdata,target=/var/lib/postgresql/data \\\n    postgres\n\nEquivalent to:\n\n    -v pgdata:/var/lib/postgresql/data\n\nBut --mount is more explicit.\n\n### Commands You Must Remember\n\n**List volumes:**\n\n    docker volume ls\n\n**Create volume:**\n\n    docker volume create myvolume\n\n**Use volume:**\n\n    docker run -v myvolume:/data\n\n**Inspect volume:**\n\n    docker volume inspect myvolume	2026-03-21 06:53:47.836+00	2	6	Day 4: Volume and postgres
15	## 1. Docker Network Drivers\n\nDocker provides different network drivers:\n\n- bridge\n- host\n- none\n- overlay\n\nEach serves a different purpose.\n\n### bridge (default)\n\n- Default network for containers\n- Containers can communicate via IP\n- Used in most local development\n\n### host\n\n- Container shares host network\n- No isolation\n- Faster, but less secure\n\n### none\n\n- No networking\n- Completely isolated container\n\n### overlay\n\n- Used in multi-host setups\n- Common in orchestration (e.g., Kubernetes, Swarm)\n\n## 2. Default Bridge Network Limitations\n\nWhen you run a container:\n\n```\ndocker run nginx\n```\n\nIt attaches to the default bridge network.\n\nProblems:\n\n- No automatic DNS resolution\n- Containers cannot refer to each other by name\n- Must use IP addresses (not reliable)\n\nExample issue:\n\nContainer A wants to call Container B\n\nYou must manually find B's IP\n\nThis is fragile because:\n\n- IP changes when container restarts\n\n## 3. User-Defined Bridge Networks\n\nSolution: create your own network.\n\n```\ndocker network create mynetwork\n```\n\nNow run containers inside it:\n\n```\ndocker run -d --name app --network mynetwork node\ndocker run -d --name db --network mynetwork mongo\n```\n\nKey Benefit\n\nDocker provides built-in DNS resolution.\n\nNow app can connect to db using:\n\n```\nmongodb://db:27017\n```\n\nNo IP needed.\n\n## 4. Container-to-Container Communication\n\nInside the same network:\n\n- Containers can talk using container names\n- Docker acts like an internal DNS server\n\nExample:\n\nApp ΓåÆ http://db:27017\n\nFlow:\n\napp container ΓåÆ Docker DNS ΓåÆ db container\n\nImportant:\n\n- Works only in same user-defined network\n- Not across different networks unless connected\n\n## 5. Exposing Services to the Host\n\nContainers are isolated by default.\n\nTo access from host:\n\n```\ndocker run -p 3000:3000 node-app\n```\n\nFormat\n\nhost_port:container_port\n\nExample:\n\nlocalhost:3000 ΓåÆ container:3000\n\nNow your browser or API client can access it.\n\nImportant Difference\n\n- Internal communication ΓåÆ uses container name\n- External access ΓåÆ uses port mapping\n\n## 6. Inspecting Networks\n\nList networks:\n\n```\ndocker network ls\n```\n\nInspect a network:\n\n```\ndocker network inspect mynetwork\n```\n\nYou can see:\n\n- connected containers\n- IP addresses\n- subnet\n- gateway\n\nConnecting Existing Containers\n\nIf container already running:\n\n```\ndocker network connect mynetwork container_name\n```\n\n## Hands-On Project\n\nGoal\n\nRun a Node app and MongoDB in the same network and connect via container name.\n\n### Step 1 ΓÇö Create Network\n\n```\ndocker network create app-network\n```\n\n### Step 2 ΓÇö Run MongoDB\n\n```\ndocker run -d \\\n  --name mongo \\\n  --network app-network \\\n  mongo\n```\n\n### Step 3 ΓÇö Run Node App\n\n```\ndocker run -d \\\n  --name node-app \\\n  --network app-network \\\n  -p 3000:3000 \\\n  node-app-image\n```\n\n### Step 4 ΓÇö Connect to MongoDB\n\nInside your Node app config:\n\n```\nmongodb://mongo:27017\n```\n\nmongo = container name\n\nDocker resolves it automatically\n\n### Step 5 ΓÇö Verify\n\nApp connects to MongoDB\n\nNo IP addresses used\n\nFully container-to-container communication\n\n## Key Commands\n\n```\ndocker network create mynetwork\ndocker network ls\ndocker network connect mynetwork container_name\ndocker network inspect mynetwork\n```\n\n---\n	2026-03-21 07:02:21.845+00	1	6	Day 5: Docker Networking
16	## 1. Why Compose Exists\n\nBefore Compose, running a multi-container app looks like this:\n\n```\ndocker run -d --name mongo mongo\ndocker run -d --name api --link mongo node-api\ndocker run -d --name frontend -p 3000:3000 react-app\n```\n\nProblems:\n\n- Too many commands\n- Hard to manage dependencies\n- No single source of truth\n- Hard to replicate across machines\n\nNow imagine real backend:\n\n- Django API\n- PostgreSQL\n- Redis\n- Celery worker\n- Nginx\n\nYou'd go insane managing manually.\n\n### Compose Solution\n\nDocker Compose lets you define everything in one file:\n\n```\ndocker-compose.yml\n```\n\nThen run:\n\n```\ndocker compose up\n```\n\nThat's it.\n\n### Mental Model\n\nCompose = Infrastructure as Code for containers\n\nYou define:\n\n- services (containers)\n- networks\n- volumes\n\n## 2. docker-compose.yml Structure\n\nBasic structure:\n\n```\nversion: "3.9"\n\nservices:\n  service_name:\n    image: some-image\n\nvolumes:\n  myvolume:\n\nnetworks:\n  mynetwork:\n```\n\n### Core Sections\n\n#### version\n\n- Defines schema version\n- Usually "3.9" or latest\n\n#### services\n\n- The most important part\n- Each service = one container\n\n#### volumes\n\n- Persistent storage\n\n#### networks\n\n- Communication layer\n\n## 3. Define Services\n\nExample:\n\n```\nservices:\n  api:\n    build: ./api \n    // Finds ./api/Dockerfile in your project and builds the image from Dockerfile\n    //Alternative: You could use `image: node:18` to pull a pre-built image instead \n\n    ports:\n          `    - "8000:8000"`\n    environment:\n          `    - DB_HOST=postgres-db`\n\n  postgres-db:\n    image: postgres:18\n    volumes:\n          `    - pgdata:/data/db`\n```\n\n### Key Fields\n\n#### image\n\n```\nimage: postgres:18\n```\n\nUse prebuilt image.\n\n#### build\n\n```\nbuild: ./api\n```\n\nBuild from Dockerfile.\n\n#### ports\n\n```\nports:\n  - "8000:8000"\n```\n\nFormat:\n\nhost:container\n\n#### environment\n\n```\nenvironment:\n  - DB_HOST=postgres-db\n```\n\nPass env variables to container.\n\n#### volumes\n\n```\nvolumes:\n  - pgdata:/data/db\n```\n\nAttach persistent storage.\n\n## 4. depends_on and Startup Order\n\nExample:\n\n```\nservices:\n  api:\n    build: ./api\n    depends_on:\n      - postgres-db\n\n  postgres-db:\n    image: postgres:18\n\n  volumes: \n      - postgres_data:/var/lib/postgresql\n\n  environment:\n      - POSTGRES_DB=${POSTGRES_DB} // fetches from .env\n      - POSTGRES_USER=${POSTGRES_USER}\n      - POSTGRES_PASSWORD=${POSTGRES_PASSWORD}\n```\n\n### What it does\n\nEnsures postgres-db starts before api\n\n### Important Reality\n\nΓ¥î It does NOT guarantee:\n\npostgres-db is ready to accept connections\n\nOnly guarantees:\n\npostgres-db container is started\n\n### Real-world solution\n\nYou need:\n\n- retry logic in app\n- or wait-for scripts\n\n## 5. docker compose Commands\n\n### Start everything\n\n```\ndocker compose up -d\n```\n\n- Builds images (if needed)\n- Creates network\n- Starts all services\n\n-d = detached mode\n\n### Stop everything\n\n```\ndocker compose down\n```\n\n- Stops containers\n- Removes containers\n- Keeps volumes (by default)\n\n### Logs\n\n```\ndocker compose logs\n```\n\nOr for specific service:\n\n```\ndocker compose logs api\n```\n\n### Check running containers\n\n```\ndocker compose ps\n```\n\nShows:\n\n- container status\n- ports\n- names\n\n## 6. docker-compose.override.yml\n\nCompose supports multiple config layers.\n\nDefault behavior:\n\n```\ndocker compose up\n```\n\nAutomatically merges:\n\n- docker-compose.yml\n- docker-compose.override.yml\n\n### Why useful?\n\nBase file:\n\n```\nservices:\n  api:\n    image: myapp\n```\n\nOverride file:\n\n```\nservices:\n  api:\n    volumes:\n      - .:/app\n    environment:\n      - DEBUG=true\n```\n\n### Use Case\n\n- Base config ΓåÆ production\n- Override ΓåÆ development\n\n## Hands-On Project\n\nGoal\n\nReact frontend + Django API + Postgres\n\n### docker-compose.yml\n\n```\nversion: "3.9"\n\nservices:\n  frontend:\n    build: ./frontend\n    ports:\n      - "3000:3000"\n    depends_on:\n      - api\n\n  api:\n    build: ./backend\n    ports:\n      - "5000:5000"\n    environment:\n      - DB_HOST=postgres-db\n    depends_on:\n      - postgres-db\n\n  postgres-db:\n    image: postgres\n    volumes:\n      - postgres:/data/db\n\nvolumes:\n  postgres-db:\n```\n\n### How It Works\n\n#### Network\n\nCompose automatically creates a network\n\nAll services are connected\n\n#### Communication\n\n- frontend ΓåÆ api via api:5000\n- api ΓåÆ postgres-db via postgres-db:27017\n\n#### Persistence\n\npostgres-db data stored in volume\n\nSurvives container restart\n\n### Run Everything\n\n```\ndocker compose up -d\n```\n\n### Stop Everything\n\n```\ndocker compose down\n```\n\n### Debug\n\n```\ndocker compose logs\ndocker compose ps\n```\n\n## Key Commands\n\n```\ndocker compose up -d\ndocker compose down\ndocker compose logs\ndocker compose ps\n```\n\n## Mental Model (Important)\n\n### Without Compose:\n\nManual chaos\n\n### With Compose:\n\nDeclarative system\n\nYou describe:\n\n- what services exist\n- how they connect\n- how they persist data\n\nDocker handles execution.\n\n## Real Backend Mapping\n\nYour typical backend:\n\n- Django ΓåÆ PostgreSQL ΓåÆ Redis ΓåÆ Celery\n\nCompose replaces:\n\n- manual docker run commands\n- manual networking\n- manual volume setup\n\n## Critical Insight (This is what most people miss)\n\nCompose is NOT just a tool.\n\nIt is:\n\nYour local production environment simulator\n\nIf your Compose setup is solid:\n\n- onboarding new dev = 1 command\n- debugging = reproducible\n- deployment = predictable	2026-03-21 07:06:13.062+00	2	6	Day 6: Docker Compose ΓÇö Basics
17	## 1. Environment Variables\n\nThere are 3 ways to handle env variables in Compose:\n\n### (A) .env file (automatic)\n\nCreate a .env file:\n\n```\nDB_HOST=postgres-db\nDB_PORT=5432\nDEBUG=true\n```\n\nUse inside docker-compose.yml:\n\n```\nservices:\n  api:\n    environment:\n      - DB_HOST=${DB_HOST}\n      - DB_PORT=${DB_PORT}\n```\n\n#### Important behavior\n\n- .env is automatically loaded\n- Only works for variable substitution in YAML, not direct injection\n\n### (B) env_file\n\n```\nservices:\n  api:\n    env_file:\n      - .env\n```\n\nNow variables go inside container environment.\n\n### (C) Direct environment\n\n```\nenvironment:\n  - DEBUG=true\n```\n\n### Mental Model\n\n- .env ΓåÆ for Compose config\n- env_file ΓåÆ for container runtime\n- environment ΓåÆ inline override\n\n## 2. Health Checks\n\nBy default:\n\nContainer running Γëá Service ready\n\nExample: DB started but not ready for connections.\n\n### Add healthcheck\n\n```\nservices:\n  postgres-db:\n    image: postgres:18\n    healthcheck:\n      test: ["CMD-SHELL", "pg_isready -U ${POSTGRES_USER}"]\n      interval: 10s\n      timeout: 5s\n      retries: 5\n```\n\n### What each field means\n\n- test ΓåÆ command to check health\n- interval ΓåÆ how often\n- timeout ΓåÆ max wait\n- retries ΓåÆ failure threshold\n\n### Why this matters\n\nYou can now build systems that:\n\n- wait for DB readiness\n- restart unhealthy containers\n- avoid race conditions\n\n## 3. Scaling Services\n\nRun multiple instances:\n\n```\ndocker compose up --scale api=3\n```\n\nNow:\n\n- api_1\n- api_2\n- api_3\n\n### Use Case\n\n- handle traffic\n- simulate load\n- test horizontal scaling\n\n### Limitation\n\n- No load balancer by default\n- You need Nginx / reverse proxy\n\n*e.g: Create `nginx.conf` in your project root.*\n```\nevents {\n    worker_connections 1024;\n}\n\nhttp {\n    upstream api_backend {\n        server api:8000;\n    }\n\n    server {\n        listen 80;\n\n        location / {\n            proxy_pass         http://api_backend;\n            proxy_set_header   Host $host;\n            proxy_set_header   X-Real-IP $remote_addr;\n            proxy_set_header   X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;\n            proxy_set_header   X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### Important Insight\n\nScaling works best for:\n\n- stateless services\n\nNOT for:\n\n- databases\n\n## 4. Compose Profiles\n\nProfiles let you run different environments.\n\n### Example\n\n```\nservices:\n  api:\n    build: .\n    profiles: ["dev"]\n\n  nginx:\n    image: nginx\n    profiles: ["prod"]\n```\n\n### Run specific profile\n\n```\ndocker compose --profile dev up\n```\n\n### Why this matters\n\nYou can define:\n\n- dev setup ΓåÆ hot reload, debug\n- prod setup ΓåÆ optimized, secure\n\n### Example Strategy\n\n- dev ΓåÆ mounts code, DEBUG=true\n- prod ΓåÆ no mounts, optimized build\n\n## 5. Build Args vs Runtime Env Vars\n\nThis is a very important distinction.\n\n### Build Args\n\nUsed during image build.\n\n```\nbuild:\n  context: .\n  args:\n    - NODE_ENV=production\n```\n\nInside Dockerfile:\n\n```\nARG NODE_ENV\n```\n\n### Runtime Environment Variables\n\nUsed when container runs.\n\n```\nenvironment:\n  - NODE_ENV=production\n```\n\n### Difference\n\n| Feature | Build Args | Env Vars |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| When used | build time | runtime |\n| Stored in image | Γ¥î | Γ¥î |\n| Accessible app | Γ¥î (after build) | Γ£à |\n\n### Mental Model\n\n- Build args ΓåÆ affect image\n- Env vars ΓåÆ affect app behavior\n\n## 6. Restart Policies\n\nControl container restart behavior.\n\n### Options\n\n- restart: no\n- restart: always\n- restart: on-failure\n- restart: unless-stopped\n\n### Meaning\n\n- no ΓåÆ never restart\n- always ΓåÆ always restart\n- on-failure ΓåÆ restart only on crash\n- unless-stopped ΓåÆ restart unless manually stopped\n\n### Real Usage\n\n- API ΓåÆ always\n- Worker ΓåÆ on-failure\n- DB ΓåÆ unless-stopped\n\n## Hands-On Project (Upgrade Day 6)\n\n### Step 1 ΓÇö Add .env\n\n```\nDB_HOST=postgres-db\nNODE_ENV=development\n```\n\n### Step 2 ΓÇö Update Compose\n\n```\nversion: "3.9"\n\nservices:\n  api:\n    build: ./backend\n    ports:\n      - "8000:8000"\n    env_file:\n      - .env\n    depends_on:\n      - postgres-db\n    restart: always\n    profiles: ["dev"]\n\n  frontend:\n    build: ./frontend\n    ports:\n      - "3000:3000"\n    depends_on:\n      - api\n    profiles: ["dev"]\n\n  postgres-db:\n    image: postgres:18\n    volumes:\n      - pgdata:/var/lib/postgres\n    restart: unless-stopped\n    healthcheck:\n        test: ["CMD-SHELL", "pg_isready -U ${POSTGRES_USER}"]\n        interval: 10s\n        timeout: 5s\n        retries: 5\n\nvolumes:\n  pgdata:\n```\n\n### Step 3 ΓÇö Run Dev Environment\n\n```\ndocker compose --profile dev up -d\n```\n\n### Step 4 ΓÇö Scale API\n\n```\ndocker compose up --scale api=3\n```\n\n### Step 5 ΓÇö Validate Config\n\n```\ndocker compose config\n```\n\nThis shows:\n\n- merged configs\n- resolved variables\n- final structure\n\n## Key Commands\n\n```\ndocker compose --profile dev up\ndocker compose up --scale api=3\ndocker compose config\n```\n\n## Mental Model (Critical)\n\nBy now you should think like this:\n\nCompose = local infrastructure layer\n\nYou control:\n\n- environment configs\n- scaling behavior\n- service health\n- restart logic\n\n## Real Backend Thinking\n\nYour future system:\n\nAPI (scaled) ΓåÆ Redis ΓåÆ Worker ΓåÆ DB\n\nWith Compose you define:\n\n- who talks to whom\n- how they start\n- how they recover\n- how they scale\n\n## Most Important Insight\n\nAt this stage:\n\nYou are no longer "running containers".\n\nYou are:\n\nDesigning a distributed system on your laptop.	2026-03-21 07:08:19.938+00	2	6	Day 7: Compose ΓÇö Advanced Patterns
18	## 1. Multi-Stage Builds: Smaller Production Images\n\n### Problem\n\nTypical Dockerfile:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM node:18\n\nWORKDIR /app\nCOPY . .\nRUN npm install\nRUN npm run build\n\nCMD ["node", "server.js"]\n```\n\nIssues:\n\n- Includes dev dependencies\n- Includes build tools\n- Image becomes huge\n\n### Solution: Multi-stage build\n\n```dockerfile\n# Stage 1: Build\nFROM node:18 AS builder\n\nWORKDIR /app\nCOPY package*.json ./\nRUN npm install\n\nCOPY . .\nRUN npm run build\n\n# Stage 2: Production\nFROM node:18-slim\n\nWORKDIR /app\nCOPY --from=builder /app/dist ./dist\nCOPY package*.json ./\nRUN npm install --only=production\n\nCMD ["node", "dist/server.js"]\n```\n\n### Core Idea of Multi-Stage Builds\n\n***A multi-stage build = multiple temporary images in one Dockerfile***\n\nEach `FROM` creates a new stage (new image layer stack).\n\n    FROM node:18 AS builder   # Stage 1\n    ...\n    FROM node:18-slim         # Stage 2\n\n≡ƒæë Think of it like:\n\n- Stage 1 ΓåÆ messy workshop (build everything)\n- Stage 2 ΓåÆ clean showroom (only final product)\n\n### ≡ƒöÑ What Happens Step-by-Step\n\n**Γ£à Stage 1: Builder (Heavy + Temporary)**\n\n    FROM node:18 AS builder\n\nThis image includes:\n\n- Full Node.js\n- Build tools\n- npm\n- Dev dependencies\n\nThen:\n\n- RUN npm install\n- RUN npm run build\n\nNow inside this stage you have:\n\n```\n/app\n  Γö£ΓöÇΓöÇ node_modules (BIG)\n  Γö£ΓöÇΓöÇ src\n  Γö£ΓöÇΓöÇ dist (FINAL BUILD OUTPUT)\n```\n\n≡ƒÆí ***Important:***\nThis stage is NOT shipped unless you explicitly use it.\n\n**Γ£à Stage 2: Production (Clean + Minimal)**\n\n    FROM node:18-slim\n\nThis is a smaller base image (no unnecessary tools).\n\nNow the key magic:\n\n    COPY --from=builder /app/dist ./dist\n\n≡ƒæë This means:\n\n**ΓÇ£Take ONLY /app/dist from the builder stage, ignore everything else.ΓÇ¥**\n\nSo:\n\nΓ¥î No dev dependencies\n\nΓ¥î No source code (if you donΓÇÖt copy it)\n\nΓ¥î No build tools\n\nΓ¥î No intermediate junk\n\nThen:\n\n    RUN npm install --only=production\n\n≡ƒæë Installs only runtime deps (much smaller)\n\n### ≡ƒôª Why Image Size Becomes Smaller\n\nWithout multi-stage:\n\nYou keep:\n\n- devDependencies\n- build tools\n- source files\n- temporary files\n\nWith multi-stage:\n\nYou keep ONLY:\n\n- compiled output (dist)\n- production dependencies\n\n## 2. Build-Time ARGs\n\nUsed to pass variables during build.\n\n```dockerfile\nARG NODE_ENV=production\nENV NODE_ENV=$NODE_ENV\n```\n\nBuild with `ARG`:\n\n```bash\ndocker build --build-arg NODE_ENV=development -t myapp .\n```\n\n### Use Cases\n\n- Switch environments\n- Toggle features\n- Choose base images\n\n### Important Difference\n\n- `ARG` is only available during build\n- `ENV` is available in the container\n\n## 3. Using Non-Root Users (Security)\n\nBy default, containers run as root, which is dangerous in production.\n\n### Solution\n\n```dockerfile\nRUN useradd -m appuser\nUSER appuser\n```\n\n### Full Example\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM node:18\n\nWORKDIR /app\nCOPY . .\n\nRUN useradd -m appuser\nUSER appuser\n\nCMD ["node", "server.js"]\n```\n\n### Why this matters\n\nIf the container is compromised, the attacker gets limited permissions.\n\n### Industry Standard\n\nAlways avoid running as root in production images.\n\n## 4. `HEALTHCHECK` Instruction\n\nDocker can monitor container health.\n\n### Example\n\n```dockerfile\nHEALTHCHECK --interval=30s --timeout=5s --retries=3 \\\n  CMD curl -f http://localhost:3000/health || exit 1\n```\n\n### Meaning\n\n- Check every 30 seconds\n- Timeout after 5 seconds\n- Fail after 3 attempts\n\n### Why this matters\n\n- Detect broken containers\n- Integrate with orchestrators\n- Auto-restart unhealthy services\n\n### Important\n\nRunning does not always mean healthy.\n\n## 5. `ONBUILD` for Base Images\n\nUsed when creating reusable base images.\n\n### Example\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM node:18\n\nONBUILD COPY . /app\nONBUILD RUN npm install\n```\n\n### What happens\n\nWhen another image uses this:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM my-base-image\n```\n\nIt automatically runs:\n\n```dockerfile\nCOPY . /app\nRUN npm install\n```\n\n### Use Cases\n\n- Company-wide base images\n- Standardizing builds\n\n### Warning\n\n`ONBUILD` can be confusing because it is implicit, so use it carefully.\n\n## 6. Optimizing Layer Cache (Very Important for CI)\n\nDocker builds use caching.\n\n### Bad Example\n\n```dockerfile\nCOPY . .\nRUN npm install\n```\n\n### Problem\n\n- Any file change invalidates the cache\n- Dependencies get reinstalled every time\n\n### Optimized Version\n\n```dockerfile\nCOPY package*.json ./\nRUN npm install\n\nCOPY . .\n```\n\n### Why this works\n\n- Dependencies change less often\n- Code changes do not invalidate `npm install`\n\n### Result\n\n- Faster builds\n- Better CI performance\n\n### Golden Rule\n\nPut the least-changing layers first.\n\n## Key Commands\n\n### Build specific stage\n\n```bash\ndocker build --target builder -t myapp .\n```\n\nUseful for:\n\n- Debugging the build stage\n- Testing intermediate images\n\n### Build with args\n\n```bash\ndocker build --build-arg NODE_ENV=production -t myapp .\n```\n\n### Clean unused images\n\n```bash\ndocker image prune\n```\n\n### Why prune matters\n\nDocker accumulates unused images and can eat disk space quickly.\n\n## Real Engineering Insights\n\n### 1. Image Size Matters\n\nSmaller images mean:\n\n- Faster pull\n- Faster deploy\n- Less bandwidth\n- Lower cost\n\n### 2. Security First\n\nAlways:\n\n- Use a non-root user\n- Use a minimal base image like `alpine` or `slim`\n\n### 3. CI/CD Optimization\n\nBad Dockerfiles lead to:\n\n- Slow builds\n- Wasted time\n\nGood Dockerfiles lead to:\n\n- Fast incremental builds\n- Efficient pipelines\n\n### 4. Production Mindset\n\nYou are not just writing Dockerfiles.\n\nYou are designing deployable artifacts.\n\n## Mental Model\n\nBy now, a Dockerfile should feel like a build pipeline.\n\nIt defines:\n\n- How your app is built\n- What goes into production\n- How secure and efficient it is\n\n## What You Should Be Able To Do Now\n\nAfter Day 8, you should be able to:\n\n- Build optimized production images\n- Reduce image size significantly\n- Secure containers\n- Speed up CI/CD builds	2026-03-21 07:17:22.932+00	2	6	Day 8: Advanced Dockerfile Techniques
19	## 1. Choosing the Right Base Image\n\nYour base image defines:\n\n- Size\n- Security surface\n- Compatibility\n\n### A. `alpine`\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM node:18-alpine\n```\n\nPros:\n\n- Very small, around 5 to 10 MB\n- Fast to pull\n- Minimal attack surface\n\nCons:\n\n- Uses `musl libc` instead of `glibc`\n- Some libraries may break\n- Debugging is harder\n\n### B. `slim`\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM node:18-slim\n```\n\nPros:\n\n- Smaller than the full image\n- More compatible than `alpine`\n- Easier debugging\n\nCons:\n\n- Larger than `alpine`\n\n### C. `distroless`\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM gcr.io/distroless/nodejs\n```\n\nPros:\n\n- No shell\n- No package manager\n- Extremely secure\n\nCons:\n\n- Hard to debug\n- No interactive access\n\n### Recommendation\n\n- Dev: full or `slim`\n- Prod: `slim` or `distroless`\n\n### Mental Model\n\nLess OS means less attack surface.\n\n## 2. Scanning Images for Vulnerabilities\n\nEven official images can have vulnerabilities.\n\n### Docker Scout\n\n```bash\ndocker scout cves myimage\n```\n\nShows:\n\n- Known CVEs\n- Severity like low, medium, high, or critical\n- Affected packages\n\n### Trivy (industry standard)\n\n```bash\ntrivy image myimage\n```\n\n### Why this matters\n\nYour image may include:\n\n- Outdated libraries\n- Known exploits\n- Security risks\n\n### Best Practice\n\nScan images in CI/CD before deployment.\n\n## 3. Secrets Management\n\nBiggest mistake beginners make:\n\n```dockerfile\nENV DB_PASSWORD=secret123\n```\n\nOr:\n\n```dockerfile\nCOPY .env /app\n```\n\n### Why this is bad\n\nSecrets become:\n\n- Part of the image\n- Visible in history\n- Accessible to anyone with the image\n\n### Correct Approach\n\nUse runtime injection:\n\n```yaml\nenvironment:\n  - DB_PASSWORD=${DB_PASSWORD}\n```\n\n### Better for production\n\n- Docker secrets\n- Environment variables from CI/CD\n- Vault systems\n\n### Rule\n\nNever bake secrets into images.\n\n## 4. Read-Only Filesystems and Capability Dropping\n\n### Read-only filesystem\n\n```bash\ndocker run --read-only myapp\n```\n\n### What this does\n\n- Container cannot write to disk\n- Prevents tampering\n\n### Use Cases\n\n- Stateless apps\n- APIs\n\n### Capability Dropping\n\nLinux containers have capabilities like:\n\n- `NET_ADMIN`\n- `SYS_ADMIN`\n\nDrop unnecessary ones:\n\n```bash\ndocker run --cap-drop ALL myapp\n```\n\n### Why this matters\n\nIt limits what an attacker can do if the container is compromised.\n\n### Principle\n\nLeast privilege.\n\n## 5. Image Signing (Docker Content Trust)\n\nEnsures image integrity.\n\n### Enable signing\n\n```bash\nexport DOCKER_CONTENT_TRUST=1\n```\n\n### What happens\n\n- Images are signed\n- Publisher is verified\n- Tampering is prevented\n\n### Why important\n\nWithout signing:\n\n- You might pull a malicious image\n\nWith signing:\n\n- You can trust the source\n\n## 6. SBOM (Software Bill of Materials)\n\nSBOM means a list of everything inside your image.\n\n### Example contents\n\n- OS packages\n- Libraries\n- Dependencies\n\n### Why this matters\n\n- Compliance, which is important in companies\n- Vulnerability tracking\n- Supply chain security\n\n### Generate SBOM\n\n```bash\ndocker scout sbom myimage\n```\n\n### Mental Model\n\nSBOM is the inventory of your image.\n\n## Key Commands\n\n### Scan vulnerabilities\n\n```bash\ndocker scout cves myimage\n```\n\n### Run read-only container\n\n```bash\ndocker run --read-only myapp\n```\n\n### Drop capabilities\n\n```bash\ndocker run --cap-drop ALL myapp\n```\n\n## Real Engineering Insights\n\n### 1. Smaller Images = Safer Images\n\n- Fewer packages\n- Fewer vulnerabilities\n- Faster deployments\n\n### 2. Security is Layers\n\nYou should combine:\n\n- Minimal base image\n- Non-root user\n- Read-only filesystem\n- Capability drop\n- Image scanning\n\n### 3. Containers Are Not Secure by Default\n\nOut of the box, containers are convenient, not secure.\n\nYou must harden them.\n\n### 4. Supply Chain Risk Is Real\n\nModern attacks target:\n\n- Dependencies\n- Base images\n- Registries\n\nNot just your code.\n\n## Mental Model\n\nAt this stage:\n\nDocker image = deployable artifact + attack surface\n\nYou must optimize:\n\n- Size\n- Security\n- Transparency\n\n## What You Should Be Able To Do Now\n\nAfter Day 9, you should be able to:\n\n- Choose correct base images\n- Scan and fix vulnerabilities\n- Secure runtime containers\n- Handle secrets properly\n- Understand supply chain risks	2026-03-21 07:18:44.638+00	2	6	Day 9: Image Optimization & Security
20	## 1. Docker Hub (Push, Pull, Public/Private)\n\nDocker Hub is the default registry for Docker.\n\n### Pull image\n\n```bash\ndocker pull nginx\n```\n\nThis actually pulls from:\n\n```text\ndocker.io/library/nginx\n```\n\n### Login\n\n```bash\ndocker login\n```\n\n### Tag image\n\n```bash\ndocker tag myapp username/myapp:latest\n```\n\n### Push image\n\n```bash\ndocker push username/myapp:latest\n```\n\n### Public vs Private\n\n- Public means anyone can pull\n- Private requires authentication\n\n### Mental Model\n\nDocker Hub is like GitHub for images.\n\n## 2. Tagging Strategy (Very Important)\n\nBad developers often use only:\n\n```text\nlatest\n```\n\nThat is not predictable.\n\nGood developers use versioning.\n\n### Common tags\n\n#### `latest`\n\n`latest` is the default tag.\n\nProblem:\n\n- Not predictable\n\n#### Semantic Versioning (`semver`)\n\n```text\nv1.0.0\nv1.1.0\nv2.0.0\n```\n\n#### Git SHA\n\n```text\nmyapp:abc1234\n```\n\n### Best Practice\n\n```text\nmyapp:1.0.0\nmyapp:1.0\nmyapp:latest\n```\n\nThe same image can have multiple tags.\n\n### Why this matters\n\n- Roll back easily\n- Track deployments\n- Avoid breaking production\n\n## 3. GitHub Container Registry (GHCR)\n\nGHCR is a registry from GitHub.\n\n### Image format\n\n```text\nghcr.io/username/myapp:tag\n```\n\n### Login\n\n```bash\ndocker login ghcr.io\n```\n\nUse a GitHub token.\n\n### Push\n\n```bash\ndocker tag myapp ghcr.io/username/myapp:1.0\ndocker push ghcr.io/username/myapp:1.0\n```\n\n### Why GHCR\n\n- Integrated with GitHub Actions\n- Better for CI/CD\n- Easier for private repositories\n\n## 4. Cloud Registries Overview\n\n### AWS ECR\n\nFrom Amazon Web Services.\n\n- Private by default\n- Integrates with AWS infrastructure\n\n### GCP Artifact Registry\n\nFrom Google Cloud Platform.\n\n- Supports multiple artifact types\n- Strong IAM integration\n\n### Azure ACR\n\nFrom Microsoft Azure.\n\n- Integrates with Azure DevOps\n- Enterprise-ready\n\n### Key Idea\n\nA registry is storage plus access control for images.\n\n## 5. Running a Local Private Registry\n\nYou can host your own registry.\n\n### Run registry\n\n```bash\ndocker run -d -p 5000:5000 --name registry registry:2\n```\n\n### Tag image\n\n```bash\ndocker tag myapp localhost:5000/myapp\n```\n\n### Push\n\n```bash\ndocker push localhost:5000/myapp\n```\n\n### Pull\n\n```bash\ndocker pull localhost:5000/myapp\n```\n\n### Use Cases\n\n- Internal company usage\n- Air-gapped environments\n- Testing\n\n## 6. Image Retention & Cleanup\n\nImages accumulate fast.\n\n### Problem\n\nUnused images mean wasted storage.\n\n### Local cleanup\n\n```bash\ndocker image prune\n```\n\n### Registry cleanup\n\nPolicies may include:\n\n- Delete old tags\n- Keep last N versions\n- Remove untagged images\n\n### Why this matters\n\n- Cost\n- Performance\n- Maintainability\n\n## Hands-On Project\n\n### Goal\n\nPush your multi-stage image to:\n\n- Docker Hub\n- GHCR\n\n### Step 1: Build image\n\n```bash\ndocker build -t myapp .\n```\n\n### Step 2: Tag for Docker Hub\n\n```bash\ndocker tag myapp username/myapp:1.0\ndocker tag myapp username/myapp:latest\n```\n\n### Step 3: Push to Docker Hub\n\n```bash\ndocker login\ndocker push username/myapp:1.0\ndocker push username/myapp:latest\n```\n\n### Step 4: Tag for GHCR\n\n```bash\ndocker tag myapp ghcr.io/username/myapp:1.0\n```\n\n### Step 5: Push to GHCR\n\n```bash\ndocker login ghcr.io\ndocker push ghcr.io/username/myapp:1.0\n```\n\n## Key Commands\n\n```bash\ndocker login\ndocker tag\ndocker push\ndocker pull <registry>/<image>\n```\n\n## Real Engineering Insights\n\n### 1. Images Are Your Deployable Units\n\nYou do not deploy code.\n\nYou deploy images.\n\n### 2. Tagging = Version Control\n\nBad tagging leads to production chaos.\n\nGood tagging leads to controlled releases.\n\n### 3. Registries Enable Teams\n\nWithout a registry:\n\n- Only local development\n\nWith a registry:\n\n- CI/CD\n- Deployments\n- Scaling\n\n### 4. Production Flow\n\nBuild -> Tag -> Push -> Deploy -> Pull\n\n## Mental Model\n\nAt this stage:\n\nRegistry = distribution layer of your system\n\n## What You Should Be Able To Do Now\n\nAfter Day 10, you should be able to:\n\n- Push images to public and private registries\n- Design tagging strategies\n- Use multiple registries\n- Manage image lifecycle	2026-03-21 07:21:49.729+00	1	6	Day 10: Working with Registries
21	## 1. GitHub Actions: Build and Push Docker Images\n\nUsing GitHub Actions, you can automate:\n\ntest -> build -> push -> deploy\n\n### Basic Workflow Structure\n\nFile:\n\n```text\n.github/workflows/docker.yml\n```\n\n### Example\n\n```yaml\nname: Docker CI\n\non:\n  push:\n    branches: [main]\n\njobs:\n  build:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n\n    steps:\n      - name: Checkout code\n        uses: actions/checkout@v4\n\n      - name: Build image\n        run: docker build -t myapp .\n```\n\n### Mental Model\n\nGitHub Actions is a server that runs your Docker commands.\n\n## 2. `docker/build-push-action`\n\nOfficial action for Docker builds.\n\n### Example\n\n```yaml\n- name: Build and push\n  uses: docker/build-push-action@v5\n  with:\n    context: .\n    push: true\n    tags: ghcr.io/username/myapp:latest\n```\n\n### Why use this\n\n- Handles `buildx` automatically\n- Supports caching\n- Supports multi-platform builds\n- Production-ready\n\n## 3. Layer Caching in CI\n\nWithout caching:\n\nEvery build becomes a full rebuild.\n\n### With caching\n\n```yaml\nwith:\n  cache-from: type=registry,ref=ghcr.io/username/myapp:cache\n  cache-to: type=registry,ref=ghcr.io/username/myapp:cache,mode=max\n```\n\n### What this does\n\n- Stores build layers\n- Reuses unchanged layers\n\n### Result\n\nFaster builds in CI.\n\n## 4. Matrix Builds (Multi-Platform)\n\nModern systems use different architectures:\n\n- `amd64` for Intel and AMD\n- `arm64` for Apple Silicon and many servers\n\n### Matrix Example\n\n```yaml\nstrategy:\n  matrix:\n    platform: [linux/amd64, linux/arm64]\n```\n\n### Build multi-platform image\n\n```yaml\nwith:\n  platforms: linux/amd64,linux/arm64\n```\n\n### Why this matters\n\nYour image works everywhere.\n\n## 5. Buildx and BuildKit\n\nThese are Docker's advanced build tools.\n\n### Enable Buildx\n\n```bash\ndocker buildx create --use\n```\n\n### Build multi-platform\n\n```bash\ndocker buildx build --platform linux/amd64,linux/arm64 -t myapp .\n```\n\n### BuildKit Features\n\n- Parallel builds\n- Better caching\n- Secret mounting\n- Faster builds\n\n### Mental Model\n\n- `Buildx` is the modern builder\n- `BuildKit` is the engine behind it\n\n## 6. Running Tests Inside Docker\n\nInstead of running tests on the host, run them inside the container.\n\n### Example\n\n```yaml\n- name: Run tests\n  run: docker run myapp pytest\n```\n\n### Why this matters\n\n- Consistent environment\n- No "works on my machine" problems\n- Production-like testing\n\n## Hands-On Project\n\n### Goal\n\nPipeline:\n\ntest -> build -> push (`ghcr.io`)\n\n### Full GitHub Actions Workflow\n\n```yaml\nname: Docker CI\n\non:\n  push:\n    branches: [main]\n\njobs:\n  docker:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n\n    steps:\n      - name: Checkout\n        uses: actions/checkout@v4\n\n      - name: Set up Docker Buildx\n        uses: docker/setup-buildx-action@v3\n\n      - name: Login to GHCR\n        run: echo "${{ secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN }}" | docker login ghcr.io -u ${{ github.actor }} --password-stdin\n\n      - name: Run tests\n        run: docker build -t myapp:test . && docker run myapp:test npm test\n\n      - name: Build and push\n        uses: docker/build-push-action@v5\n        with:\n          context: .\n          push: true\n          tags: ghcr.io/${{ github.repository }}/myapp:latest\n          cache-from: type=registry,ref=ghcr.io/${{ github.repository }}/myapp:cache\n          cache-to: type=registry,ref=ghcr.io/${{ github.repository }}/myapp:cache,mode=max\n```\n\n### What Happens\n\n- Code is pushed to `main`\n- GitHub Actions runs\n- Tests execute inside Docker\n- Image is built\n- Image is pushed to GHCR\n\n## Key Commands\n\n### Create builder\n\n```bash\ndocker buildx create\n```\n\n### Build multi-platform\n\n```bash\ndocker buildx build\n```\n\n### Inspect image manifest\n\n```bash\ndocker manifest inspect myapp\n```\n\n## Real Engineering Insights\n\n### 1. CI/CD = Automation Layer\n\nWithout CI/CD:\n\n- Manual\n- Error-prone\n\nWith CI/CD:\n\n- Consistent\n- Repeatable\n\n### 2. Docker and CI/CD Are a Powerful Combo\n\n- Build once\n- Run anywhere\n- Deploy everywhere\n\n### 3. Tests Must Run in Containers\n\nThis ensures the same environment as production.\n\n### 4. Multi-Platform Is Future-Proofing\n\nIgnoring it means your app can break on ARM.\n\n### 5. Caching Saves Time and Money\n\nFaster builds mean:\n\n- Lower CI costs\n- Faster feedback\n\n## Final Mental Model\n\nAt this stage:\n\nCode -> CI -> Docker Image -> Registry -> Deployment\n\n## What You Can Do Now (This Is Big)\n\nAfter Day 11, you can:\n\n- Automate Docker builds\n- Push images from CI\n- Run tests inside containers\n- Support multiple architectures\n- Optimize pipelines\n\nThis is the point where you are job-ready for backend and DevOps roles.	2026-03-21 07:24:34.883+00	2	6	Day 11: Docker in CI/CD Pipelines
22	Up until now:\n\n    Docker -> single machine\n\nNow:\n\n    Swarm -> multiple machines acting as one system\n\n\n## 1. Why Orchestration?\n\nRunning containers manually works until:\n\n- Traffic increases\n- Containers crash\n- You need zero-downtime deploys\n\n### Problems Without Orchestration\n\n- Manual scaling\n- Manual restarts\n- Downtime during deploy\n\n### What Orchestration Solves\n\n#### Scaling\n\nRun multiple instances automatically.\n\n#### Self-healing\n\nIf a container crashes, it restarts automatically.\n\n#### Rolling updates\n\nDeploy a new version without downtime.\n\n### Mental Model\n\nAn orchestrator is the system that manages containers for you.\n\n## 2. Swarm Mode: Managers and Workers\n\nSwarm turns Docker into a cluster system.\n\n### Node Types\n\n#### Manager node\n\n- Controls the cluster\n- Schedules containers\n- Maintains state\n\n#### Worker node\n\n- Runs containers\n- Executes tasks\n\n### Architecture\n\n- Manager decides\n- Workers execute\n\n### Important\n\nYou can have multiple managers for high availability.\n\n## 3. Initializing a Swarm\n\n### Initialize\n\n```bash\ndocker swarm init\n```\n\n### Output\n\nYou will get a join token:\n\n```bash\ndocker swarm join --token <token> <ip>:2377\n```\n\n### Join worker node\n\n```bash\ndocker swarm join --token <token> <manager-ip>:2377\n```\n\n### Check nodes\n\n```bash\ndocker node ls\n```\n\n### Local Setup\n\nEven on your laptop:\n\n1 node = manager + worker\n\n## 4. Services vs Containers\n\nIn Swarm, you do not run containers directly.\n\nYou define services.\n\n### Create service\n\n```bash\ndocker service create --name myapp -p 3000:3000 nginx\n```\n\n### What is a service?\n\nThe desired state of your application.\n\nExample:\n\n"I want 3 replicas of my API running."\n\n### Replicas\n\n```bash\ndocker service create --replicas 3 nginx\n```\n\nNow Docker ensures 3 containers are always running.\n\n### Constraints\n\nControl where containers run:\n\n```bash\ndocker service create \\\n  --constraint 'node.role==worker' \\\n  nginx\n```\n\n### Mental Model\n\n- Container = instance\n- Service = definition\n\n## 5. Rolling Updates and Rollbacks\n\n### Update service\n\n```bash\ndocker service update --image nginx:latest myapp\n```\n\n### What happens\n\nInstead of:\n\nStop all -> start new\n\nSwarm does:\n\nStop 1 -> update -> start -> repeat\n\n### Controlled update\n\n```bash\ndocker service update \\\n  --update-parallelism 1 \\\n  --update-delay 10s \\\n  myapp\n```\n\n### Rollback\n\n```bash\ndocker service update --rollback myapp\n```\n\n### Why this matters\n\nZero-downtime deployments.\n\n## 6. Swarm Networking (Overlay and Ingress)\n\n### Overlay network\n\nUsed for multi-node communication.\n\n```bash\ndocker network create --driver overlay mynet\n```\n\n### What it does\n\n- Connects containers across nodes\n- Provides internal DNS\n\n### Ingress network\n\nDefault load balancer.\n\nExample:\n\n```bash\ndocker service create -p 3000:3000 nginx\n```\n\n### What happens\n\nAny node on port `3000` gets routed to the container.\n\n### This is huge\n\nBuilt-in load balancing.\n\n## Hands-On Project\n\n### Goal\n\n- Initialize swarm\n- Deploy a service with 3 replicas\n- Perform a rolling update\n\n### Step 1: Init swarm\n\n```bash\ndocker swarm init\n```\n\n### Step 2: Create service\n\n```bash\ndocker service create \\\n  --name myapp \\\n  --replicas 3 \\\n  -p 3000:80 \\\n  nginx\n```\n\n### Step 3: Check services\n\n```bash\ndocker service ls\n```\n\n### Step 4: Check running containers\n\n```bash\ndocker service ps myapp\n```\n\n### Step 5: Simulate failure\n\nKill a container:\n\n```bash\ndocker ps\ndocker kill <container_id>\n```\n\n### Result\n\nSwarm automatically creates a new container.\n\nThis is self-healing.\n\n### Step 6: Rolling update\n\n```bash\ndocker service update --image nginx:alpine myapp\n```\n\nWatch:\n\n```bash\ndocker service ps myapp\n```\n\n### Step 7: Rollback\n\n```bash\ndocker service update --rollback myapp\n```\n\n## Key Commands\n\n```bash\ndocker swarm init\ndocker service create\ndocker service update\ndocker service ls\n```\n\n## Real Engineering Insights\n\n### 1. You Stop Managing Containers\n\nInstead of:\n\nRun container\n\nYou say:\n\nMaintain N replicas\n\n### 2. Desired State System\n\nSwarm constantly ensures:\n\nActual state = desired state\n\n### 3. Built-in Load Balancing\n\nYou do not need Nginx for basic routing.\n\nSwarm handles:\n\nRequest -> distribute -> containers\n\n### 4. Self-Healing Is Critical\n\nWithout it:\n\nCrash = downtime\n\nWith it:\n\nCrash -> auto recovery\n\n### 5. Swarm vs Kubernetes\n\n#### Swarm\n\n- Simple\n- Easy to learn\n- Less powerful\n\n#### Kubernetes\n\n- Complex\n- Industry standard\n- More control\n\n## Mental Model\n\nAt this stage:\n\n- Docker runs containers\n- Compose manages local multi-container apps\n- Swarm manages distributed systems	2026-03-21 07:29:04.001+00	2	6	Day 12: Docker Swarm - Container Orchestration
23	## Orchestration and Production\n\n### Topics\n\n- **Docker Stacks: Compose files for Swarm**\n- **`deploy` key: replicas, resources, `restart_policy`, `update_config`**\n- **Swarm secrets: securely injecting sensitive data**\n- **Swarm configs for non-sensitive config files**\n- **Placement constraints and node labels**\n- **Global services vs replicated services**\n\n## Hands-On Project\n\nDeploy a full stack, frontend + API + DB, using `docker stack deploy` with secrets for the DB password.\n\n## Key Commands For This Day\n\n```bash\ndocker stack deploy\ndocker secret create\ndocker config create\ndocker stack ps\n```\n\nNow you are moving from basic orchestration to production-grade deployment.\n\nDay 12 was "run services".\n\nDay 13 is "deploy real systems safely".\n\n# Day 13: Docker Stacks and Swarm Secrets\n\n## 1. Docker Stacks (Compose for Swarm)\n\nIn Swarm, you do not use:\n\n```bash\ndocker compose up\n```\n\nYou use:\n\n```bash\ndocker stack deploy\n```\n\n### What is a stack?\n\nA stack is a group of services deployed together.\n\nIt uses a Compose file, but with Swarm-specific features.\n\n### Example\n\n```yaml\nversion: "3.9"\n\nservices:\n  api:\n    image: myapp\n    deploy:\n      replicas: 3\n\n  db:\n    image: postgres\n```\n\n### Deploy stack\n\n```bash\ndocker stack deploy -c docker-compose.yml myapp\n```\n\n### Mental Model\n\n- Compose = local development\n- Stack = production deployment\n\n## 2. `deploy` Key (Swarm-only Features)\n\nThe `deploy` section is ignored in Compose, but used in Swarm.\n\n### Example\n\n```yaml\nservices:\n  api:\n    image: myapp\n    deploy:\n      replicas: 3\n      restart_policy:\n        condition: on-failure\n      resources:\n        limits:\n          cpus: "0.5"\n          memory: 512M\n      update_config:\n        parallelism: 1\n        delay: 10s\n```\n\n### Key Options\n\n#### `replicas`\n\nNumber of container instances.\n\n#### `restart_policy`\n\nWhen to restart the container.\n\n#### `resources`\n\nCPU and memory limits.\n\n#### `update_config`\n\nControls rolling updates:\n\n- `parallelism` = how many update at once\n- `delay` = wait time between updates\n\n### Why this matters\n\nControl over scaling, reliability, and updates.\n\n## 3. Swarm Secrets (Critical)\n\nNever do this:\n\n```yaml\nenvironment:\n  - DB_PASSWORD=secret123\n```\n\nInstead, use secrets.\n\n### Create secret\n\n```bash\ndocker secret create db_password ./password.txt\n```\n\n### Use in service\n\n```yaml\nservices:\n  db:\n    image: postgres\n    secrets:\n      - db_password\n\nsecrets:\n  db_password:\n    external: true\n```\n\n### Inside container\n\nThe secret is available at:\n\n```text\n/run/secrets/db_password\n```\n\n### Why this is powerful\n\n- Not stored in image\n- Not visible in logs\n- Encrypted in transit\n\n### Mental Model\n\nSecrets = secure runtime-only data\n\n## 4. Swarm Configs (Non-sensitive Data)\n\nConfigs are like secrets, but for non-sensitive files.\n\n### Example\n\n```bash\ndocker config create app_config ./config.json\n```\n\n### Use in service\n\n```yaml\nservices:\n  api:\n    image: myapp\n    configs:\n      - app_config\n\nconfigs:\n  app_config:\n    external: true\n```\n\n### Use Cases\n\n- Config files\n- App settings\n- Nginx config\n\n### Difference\n\n- Secrets = sensitive data like passwords\n- Configs = non-sensitive settings\n\n## 5. Placement Constraints and Node Labels\n\nYou can control where services run.\n\n### Add label to node\n\n```bash\ndocker node update --label-add role=db node1\n```\n\n### Use constraint\n\n```yaml\ndeploy:\n  placement:\n    constraints:\n      - node.labels.role == db\n```\n\n### Use Case\n\n- DB on a specific node with storage\n- API on any node\n\n### Why this matters\n\nIt gives you control over resource placement in the cluster.\n\n## 6. Global vs Replicated Services\n\n### Replicated (default)\n\n```yaml\ndeploy:\n  replicas: 3\n```\n\nRuns a fixed number of containers.\n\n### Global\n\n```yaml\ndeploy:\n  mode: global\n```\n\nRuns 1 container per node.\n\n### Examples\n\n- Monitoring agent\n- Logging service\n\n### Mental Model\n\n- Replicated = scale by count\n- Global = run everywhere\n\n## Hands-On Project\n\n### Goal\n\nDeploy:\n\n- Frontend\n- API\n- Database\n\nSecure the DB password with secrets.\n\n### Step 1: Create secret\n\n```bash\necho "mypassword" | docker secret create db_password -\n```\n\n### Step 2: Create Compose file\n\n```yaml\nversion: "3.9"\n\nservices:\n  frontend:\n    image: frontend-app\n    ports:\n      - "3000:3000"\n\n  api:\n    image: backend-app\n    depends_on:\n      - db\n    environment:\n      - DB_HOST=db\n    deploy:\n      replicas: 2\n\n  db:\n    image: postgres\n    secrets:\n      - db_password\n    environment:\n      - POSTGRES_PASSWORD_FILE=/run/secrets/db_password\n    volumes:\n      - db_data:/var/lib/postgresql/data\n\nsecrets:\n  db_password:\n    external: true\n\nvolumes:\n  db_data:\n```\n\n### Step 3: Deploy stack\n\n```bash\ndocker stack deploy -c docker-compose.yml myapp\n```\n\n### Step 4: Check services\n\n```bash\ndocker stack ps myapp\n```\n\n### Step 5: Verify\n\n- Frontend is accessible\n- API connects to DB\n- Password is not exposed\n\n## Key Commands\n\n```bash\ndocker stack deploy\ndocker secret create\ndocker config create\ndocker stack ps\n```\n\n## Real Engineering Insights\n\n### 1. Secrets Are Mandatory in Production\n\nWithout secrets:\n\nCredentials leak easily\n\nWith secrets:\n\nSecure and controlled access\n\n### 2. Stack = Production Deployment Unit\n\nInstead of running containers manually, you define the entire system in one file.\n\n### 3. Infrastructure Becomes Declarative\n\nYou describe the desired system.\n\nSwarm makes it real.\n\n### 4. Separation of Concerns\n\n- Image = code\n- Config = behavior\n- Secrets = sensitive data\n\n### 5. Real System Thinking\n\nYou now think like:\n\n- Where should this run?\n- How should it scale?\n- How do I secure it?\n\n## Mental Model\n\nAt this stage:\n\n- Dockerfile = build artifact\n- Compose = local system\n- Swarm = orchestration\n- Stack = production deployment	2026-03-21 07:30:10.716+00	1	6	Day 13: Docker Stacks and Swarm Secrets
24	## 1. Docker vs Kubernetes - When to Use Which\n\n### Docker (what you've been using)\n\n- Build images\n- Run containers\n- Simple orchestration with Swarm or Compose\n\n### Kubernetes (`K8s`)\n\n- Manages containers at scale\n- Handles networking, scaling, and recovery\n- Industry standard\n\n### Key Difference\n\n- Docker runs containers\n- Kubernetes manages containers across clusters\n\n### When to Use\n\n#### Use Docker alone\n\n- Local development\n- Small apps\n- Simple deployments\n\n#### Use Kubernetes\n\n- Production systems\n- Scaling required\n- Microservices architecture\n\n### Reality\n\nDocker plus Kubernetes is the real-world setup.\n\n## 2. Kubernetes Core Objects\n\nKubernetes does not use containers directly.\n\nIt uses higher-level abstractions.\n\n### Pod (smallest unit)\n\nA pod is one or more containers.\n\nExample:\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: v1\nkind: Pod\nmetadata:\n  name: myapp\nspec:\n  containers:\n    - name: app\n      image: myapp:latest\n```\n\n### Deployment (most important)\n\nA deployment manages pods.\n\nIt handles:\n\n- Scaling\n- Updates\n- Self-healing\n\nExample:\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: Deployment\nmetadata:\n  name: myapp\nspec:\n  replicas: 3\n  selector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: myapp\n  template:\n    metadata:\n      labels:\n        app: myapp\n    spec:\n      containers:\n        - name: app\n          image: myapp:latest\n```\n\n### Service (networking)\n\nA service exposes pods.\n\nTypes:\n\n- `ClusterIP` for internal traffic\n- `NodePort` for external access\n- `LoadBalancer` for cloud environments\n\nExample:\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: v1\nkind: Service\nmetadata:\n  name: myapp-service\nspec:\n  type: NodePort\n  selector:\n    app: myapp\n  ports:\n    - port: 80\n      targetPort: 3000\n      nodePort: 30007\n```\n\n### Ingress (advanced routing)\n\nAn ingress is the HTTP routing layer.\n\n- Domain-based routing\n- SSL termination\n\n### Mental Model\n\nContainer -> Pod -> Deployment -> Service -> Ingress\n\n## 3. `kubectl` Basics (Docker Mindset)\n\n`kubectl` is the CLI for Kubernetes.\n\n### Apply config\n\n```bash\nkubectl apply -f deployment.yaml\n```\n\n### Check pods\n\n```bash\nkubectl get pods\n```\n\n### Describe resource\n\n```bash\nkubectl describe pod myapp\n```\n\n### Logs\n\n```bash\nkubectl logs <pod-name>\n```\n\n### Mapping from Docker\n\n| Docker | Kubernetes |\n| --- | --- |\n| `docker run` | `kubectl apply` |\n| container | pod |\n| service (Swarm) | deployment |\n| port mapping | service |\n\n## 4. Your Docker Images Run Unchanged\n\nThis is huge:\n\nKubernetes does not replace Docker images.\n\nYou still:\n\n- Build\n- Tag\n- Push image\n\nKubernetes just pulls it:\n\n```yaml\nimage: ghcr.io/username/myapp:1.0\n```\n\n### Mental Model\n\n- Docker = packaging\n- Kubernetes = execution system\n\n## 5. Local Kubernetes (`Minikube` / `Kind`)\n\nYou do not need a cloud cluster.\n\n### Minikube\n\n- Runs a single-node cluster locally\n\n```bash\nminikube start\n```\n\n### Kind (`Kubernetes in Docker`)\n\n- Runs a cluster inside Docker\n\n### Recommendation\n\n- `Minikube` is beginner-friendly\n- `Kind` is faster and more dev-focused\n\n## 6. Helm Charts (Compose for Kubernetes)\n\nHelm is like:\n\n`docker-compose.yml` -> Helm chart\n\n### What Helm does\n\n- Templates Kubernetes configs\n- Manages versions\n- Simplifies deployments\n\n### Example\n\nInstead of writing huge YAML files:\n\n```bash\nhelm install myapp ./chart\n```\n\n### Mental Model\n\nHelm is the package manager for Kubernetes.\n\n## Hands-On Project\n\n### Goal\n\n- Deploy your Docker image\n- Run it in Kubernetes\n- Expose it via `NodePort`\n\n### Step 1: Start Minikube\n\n```bash\nminikube start\n```\n\n### Step 2: Create deployment\n\nFile:\n\n```text\ndeployment.yaml\n```\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: Deployment\nmetadata:\n  name: myapp\nspec:\n  replicas: 2\n  selector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: myapp\n  template:\n    metadata:\n      labels:\n        app: myapp\n    spec:\n      containers:\n        - name: myapp\n          image: ghcr.io/username/myapp:latest\n          ports:\n            - containerPort: 3000\n```\n\n### Step 3: Apply deployment\n\n```bash\nkubectl apply -f deployment.yaml\n```\n\n### Step 4: Expose service\n\nFile:\n\n```text\nservice.yaml\n```\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: v1\nkind: Service\nmetadata:\n  name: myapp-service\nspec:\n  type: NodePort\n  selector:\n    app: myapp\n  ports:\n    - port: 80\n      targetPort: 3000\n      nodePort: 30007\n```\n\n### Step 5: Apply service\n\n```bash\nkubectl apply -f service.yaml\n```\n\n### Step 6: Access app\n\n```bash\nminikube service myapp-service\n```\n\n## Key Commands\n\n```bash\nkubectl apply -f\nkubectl get pods\nkubectl describe\nkubectl logs\n```\n\n## Real Engineering Insights\n\n### 1. Kubernetes Is Declarative\n\nYou do not say:\n\nRun container\n\nYou say:\n\nI want 3 replicas running\n\n### 2. Self-Healing by Default\n\n- Pod crashes -> recreated\n- Node dies -> rescheduled\n\n### 3. Networking Is Abstracted\n\nYou do not deal with IPs directly.\n\nThe service handles routing.\n\n### 4. Scaling Is Built-In\n\n```bash\nkubectl scale deployment myapp --replicas=5\n```\n\n### 5. Industry Reality\n\n#### Swarm\n\n- Good for learning\n\n#### Kubernetes\n\n- Used in production everywhere\n\n## Mental Model\n\nAt this stage:\n\n- Docker builds images\n- Registry stores images\n- Kubernetes runs and manages them at scale	2026-03-21 07:33:13.799+00	2	6	Day 14: Kubernetes Primer (from Docker Lens)
25	## Orchestration and Production\n\n## Topics\n\n- **Docker stats and resource constraints (`--memory` `--cpus`**)\n- **`cgroups` and namespaces under the hood**\n- **Logging drivers: `json-file`, `syslog`, `fluentd`**\n- **Centralized logging with Loki plus Grafana via Compose**\n- **Metrics with Prometheus plus `cAdvisor`**\n- **Container health monitoring best practices**\n\n## Hands-On Project\n\nSet up a Compose stack with `cAdvisor` + Prometheus + Grafana, and monitor your app containers.\n\n## Key Commands For This Day\n\n```bash\ndocker stats\ndocker run --memory\ndocker run --log-driver\ndocker events\n```\n\nThis is where you move from running systems to understanding systems in production.\n\nIf you cannot observe it, you cannot debug it.\n\n## 1. Docker Stats and Resource Constraints\n\n### Real-time container metrics\n\n```bash\ndocker stats\n```\n\nShows:\n\n- CPU usage\n- Memory usage\n- Network I/O\n- Disk I/O\n\n### Limit resources\n\n#### Memory limit\n\n```bash\ndocker run --memory="512m" myapp\n```\n\n#### CPU limit\n\n```bash\ndocker run --cpus="0.5" myapp\n```\n\n### Why this matters\n\nWithout limits, one container can crash your entire system.\n\n### Mental Model\n\nContainers share host resources, so you must control usage.\n\n## 2. `cgroups` and Namespaces (Under the Hood)\n\nThis is the real foundation of Docker.\n\n### Namespaces -> isolation\n\nThey isolate:\n\n- Processes\n- Network\n- Filesystem\n- Users\n\n### `cgroups` -> resource control\n\nThey limit:\n\n- CPU\n- Memory\n- I/O\n\n### Together\n\n- Namespaces = isolation\n- `cgroups` = control\n\n### Why you should care\n\nThis explains:\n\n- Why containers are lightweight\n- How limits actually work\n\n## 3. Logging Drivers\n\nBy default, Docker logs use:\n\n`json-file`\n\n### `json-file` (default)\n\n- Stores logs locally\n- Accessible via:\n\n```bash\ndocker logs container_id\n```\n\n### `syslog`\n\n```bash\ndocker run --log-driver=syslog myapp\n```\n\nSends logs to the system logger.\n\n### `fluentd`\n\n```bash\ndocker run --log-driver=fluentd myapp\n```\n\nSends logs to a centralized system.\n\n### Why this matters\n\nProduction systems need centralized logging.\n\n## 4. Centralized Logging (Loki + Grafana)\n\nInstead of checking logs per container, you aggregate logs.\n\n### Stack\n\n- Loki = log storage\n- Grafana = visualization\n\n### Compose Example\n\n```yaml\nversion: "3.9"\n\nservices:\n  loki:\n    image: grafana/loki\n    ports:\n      - "3100:3100"\n\n  grafana:\n    image: grafana/grafana\n    ports:\n      - "3000:3000"\n```\n\n### Flow\n\nContainer logs -> Loki -> Grafana dashboard\n\n### Result\n\n- Search logs\n- Filter by service\n- Visualize errors\n\n## 5. Metrics (Prometheus + `cAdvisor`)\n\n### `cAdvisor`\n\nCollects container metrics.\n\nRun:\n\n```bash\ndocker run -d \\\n  --name cadvisor \\\n  -p 8080:8080 \\\n  gcr.io/cadvisor/cadvisor\n```\n\n### Prometheus\n\nStores and queries metrics.\n\n### Flow\n\nContainers -> `cAdvisor` -> Prometheus -> Grafana\n\n### Why this matters\n\nYou can monitor:\n\n- CPU spikes\n- Memory leaks\n- Traffic patterns\n\n## 6. Container Health Monitoring Best Practices\n\n### 1. Use `HEALTHCHECK`\n\nInside your Dockerfile:\n\n```dockerfile\nHEALTHCHECK CMD curl -f http://localhost:3000/health || exit 1\n```\n\n### 2. Use orchestration checks\n\n- Swarm health checks\n- Kubernetes probes\n\n### 3. Monitor metrics\n\n- CPU\n- Memory\n- Response time\n\n### 4. Alerting\n\nDo not just monitor, react.\n\n### 5. Log everything important\n\n- Requests\n- Errors\n- Failures\n\n## Hands-On Project\n\n### Goal\n\nSet up a monitoring stack with:\n\n- `cAdvisor`\n- Prometheus\n- Grafana\n\n### `docker-compose.yml`\n\n```yaml\nversion: "3.9"\n\nservices:\n  cadvisor:\n    image: gcr.io/cadvisor/cadvisor\n    ports:\n      - "8080:8080"\n    volumes:\n      - /:/rootfs:ro\n      - /var/run:/var/run:ro\n      - /sys:/sys:ro\n      - /var/lib/docker/:/var/lib/docker:ro\n\n  prometheus:\n    image: prom/prometheus\n    ports:\n      - "9090:9090"\n\n  grafana:\n    image: grafana/grafana\n    ports:\n      - "3000:3000"\n```\n\n### Step 1: Start stack\n\n```bash\ndocker compose up -d\n```\n\n### Step 2: Access\n\n- `cAdvisor` -> `http://localhost:8080`\n- Prometheus -> `http://localhost:9090`\n- Grafana -> `http://localhost:3000`\n\n### Step 3: Connect Grafana\n\n- Add Prometheus as a data source\n- Create dashboards\n\n### Step 4: Monitor your app\n\nWatch:\n\n- CPU usage\n- Memory usage\n- Container health\n\n## Key Commands\n\n```bash\ndocker stats\ndocker run --memory\ndocker run --log-driver\ndocker events\n```\n\n### `docker events`\n\nShows:\n\n- Container start and stop\n- Crashes\n- Restarts\n\n## Real Engineering Insights\n\n### 1. Logs vs Metrics vs Events\n\n- Logs = what happened\n- Metrics = how the system behaves\n- Events = lifecycle changes\n\n### 2. Observability Does Not Equal Monitoring\n\n#### Monitoring\n\nKnow something is wrong.\n\n#### Observability\n\nUnderstand why it is wrong.\n\n### 3. Production Rule\n\nIf it is not monitored, it will fail silently.\n\n### 4. Debugging in Production\n\nYou cannot rely on:\n\n`print()` debugging\n\nYou must rely on:\n\n- Logs\n- Metrics\n- Traces\n\n## Mental Model\n\nAt this stage:\n\nApp -> Container -> Metrics + Logs -> Monitoring Stack\n\n## What You Can Do Now\n\nAfter Day 15, you can:\n\n- Monitor container performance\n- Centralize logs\n- Detect failures early\n- Debug production systems\n\nIf you want next:\n\nDay 16 = Production Best Practices + Deployment Strategies (blue-green, canary, and more)\n\nThat is the final step into real-world system design.	2026-03-21 07:33:32.648+00	1	6	Day 15: Monitoring and Observability
26	## 1. 12-Factor App (Applied to Docker)\n\nThe Twelve-Factor App defines how modern apps should behave.\n\n### Key principles translated to Docker\n\n#### Config via environment\n\nDo not hardcode configs.\n\n```bash\ndocker run -e DB_HOST=db myapp\n```\n\n#### Stateless containers\n\nContainers should not store data.\n\nUse:\n\n- Volumes\n- External DB\n\n#### Logs as streams\n\nLogs should go to `stdout` and `stderr`.\n\nThat lets Docker capture them:\n\n```bash\ndocker logs\n```\n\n#### One process per container\n\nAvoid running multiple services in one container.\n\n### Mental Model\n\nDocker works best when the app follows 12-factor principles.\n\n## 2. Graceful Shutdown (`SIGTERM` Handling)\n\nWhen stopping a container:\n\n```bash\ndocker stop myapp\n```\n\nDocker sends:\n\n`SIGTERM` -> wait -> `SIGKILL`\n\n### Problem\n\nIf not handled:\n\n- Requests get cut mid-way\n- Data corruption can happen\n\n### Solution\n\nHandle `SIGTERM` in your app.\n\n### Node.js Example\n\n```javascript\nprocess.on("SIGTERM", () => {\n  console.log("Shutting down...");\n  server.close(() => {\n    process.exit(0);\n  });\n});\n```\n\n### Python Example\n\n```python\nimport signal\n\ndef shutdown(sig, frame):\n    print("Shutting down")\n    exit(0)\n\nsignal.signal(signal.SIGTERM, shutdown)\n```\n\n### Why this matters\n\nClean shutdown means a stable system.\n\n## 3. Zero-Downtime Deployment Strategies\n\n### Bad deployment\n\nStop old -> start new -> downtime\n\n### Rolling update\n\n- Update one container at a time\n- No downtime\n\nThis is the default idea in Swarm and Kubernetes.\n\n### Blue-Green Deployment\n\n- Blue = current version\n- Green = new version\n\nSwitch traffic instantly.\n\n### Canary Deployment\n\nRelease to a small percentage of users first, then expand.\n\n### Mental Model\n\nDeploy without users noticing.\n\n## 4. Reverse Proxy Pattern (`Traefik` / `Nginx`)\n\nInstead of exposing every service directly:\n\nApp -> direct ports\n\nUse a reverse proxy.\n\n### Tools\n\n- `Traefik` for modern, auto-configured setups\n- `Nginx` for classic, powerful setups\n\n### What a reverse proxy does\n\nIncoming request -> route -> correct container\n\nExample:\n\n- `/api` -> backend\n- `/` -> frontend\n\n### Why `Traefik` is popular\n\n- Auto-detects containers\n- Supports dynamic routing\n- Integrates with Docker labels\n\n### Sidecar Pattern\n\nApp container plus proxy container.\n\n## 5. Docker Production Checklist\n\n### Must-have\n\n- Health checks\n- Resource limits\n- Logging setup\n- Monitoring\n- Restart policies\n\n### Security\n\n- Run as non-root\n- Use minimal base images\n- Do not store secrets in images\n\n### Reliability\n\n- Volumes for data\n- Backups\n- Scaling strategy\n\n### Deployment\n\n- CI/CD pipeline\n- Versioned images\n- Rollback plan\n\n### Mental Model\n\nProduction = reliability + observability + security\n\n## 6. Debugging Running Containers (Safely)\n\n### Enter container\n\n```bash\ndocker exec -it container_id sh\n```\n\n### Copy files\n\n```bash\ndocker cp container_id:/app/log.txt .\n```\n\n### Check file changes\n\n```bash\ndocker diff container_id\n```\n\n### `docker commit` (dangerous)\n\n```bash\ndocker commit container_id debug-image\n```\n\n### When not to use `commit`\n\nNever use it in production pipelines.\n\nWhy:\n\n- Breaks reproducibility\n- Is not version-controlled\n\n### Proper way\n\nFix the Dockerfile, then rebuild the image.\n\n## Hands-On Project\n\n### Goal\n\n- Add reverse proxy with `Traefik`\n- Implement `SIGTERM` handling\n- Route traffic automatically\n\n### Example Compose with `Traefik`\n\n```yaml\nversion: "3.9"\n\nservices:\n  traefik:\n    image: traefik:v2.10\n    command:\n      - "--api.insecure=true"\n      - "--providers.docker=true"\n    ports:\n      - "80:80"\n      - "8080:8080"\n    volumes:\n      - /var/run/docker.sock:/var/run/docker.sock\n\n  app:\n    image: myapp\n    labels:\n      - "traefik.http.routers.app.rule=Host(`localhost`)"\n      - "traefik.http.services.app.loadbalancer.server.port=3000"\n```\n\n### What happens\n\nRequest -> `Traefik` -> correct container\n\n### Add `SIGTERM` handling\n\nModify your app to:\n\n- Catch `SIGTERM`\n- Stop gracefully\n\n### Test it\n\n```bash\ndocker stop app\n```\n\nObserve:\n\n- No abrupt crash\n- Clean shutdown\n\n## Key Commands\n\n```bash\ndocker exec\ndocker cp\ndocker diff\ndocker commit\n```\n\n## Real Engineering Insights\n\n### 1. Containers Are Not Enough\n\nRunning containers is easy.\n\nRunning them in production is hard.\n\n### 2. Most Failures Are Not Code\n\nThey are often:\n\n- Bad deployments\n- Missing monitoring\n- Improper shutdown\n\n### 3. Reverse Proxy Is a Central Piece\n\nAll traffic goes through one entry point.\n\n### 4. Reproducibility Is Everything\n\nThe same image should give the same behavior everywhere.\n\n### 5. Think in Systems\n\nNot:\n\n"My container"\n\nBut:\n\n"Entire system behavior"\n\n## Final Mental Model (Complete Journey)\n\n- Dockerfile -> image\n- Compose -> local system\n- Swarm and Kubernetes -> orchestration\n- CI/CD -> automation\n- Monitoring -> visibility\n- Production patterns -> reliability\n\n## What You Are Now\n\nAfter Day 16, you can:\n\n- Design production-ready systems\n- Deploy without downtime\n- Debug live systems safely\n- Think like a backend and DevOps engineer	2026-03-21 07:54:00.716+00	1	6	Day 16: Production Patterns & Best Practices
\.


